Replication
Lesson 1 — Why Replication Exists
Let's start with a question that sounds trivial:
Why do we need more than one copy of a database?
A common answer is:
"For high availability."
That's correct, but incomplete.
In production distributed databases, replication solves several different problems, and understanding which problem you're solving is more important than knowing the word "replication."
________________________________________
1. First principles: what is replication?
Suppose we have:
Application
     |
     v
 Database A
Database A contains:
Users
Orders
Payments
Products
...
Every piece of data has one physical representation on Database A.
Now suppose we create another database:
                +--> Database A
Application ---|
                +--> Database B
and maintain copies of the same logical data:
Database A
users:
  id=101, name=Alice

Database B
users:
  id=101, name=Alice
That is replication.
More precisely:
Replication is maintaining multiple copies of logically related data on different storage/compute nodes.
The important word is multiple.
Without replication:
logical data X
      |
      v
one physical copy
With replication:
             +--> physical copy 1
logical X ---+
             +--> physical copy 2
             +--> physical copy 3
Already, an important distinction appears:
Replication is not the same as partitioning.
We'll need this distinction constantly.
________________________________________
2. Replication vs partitioning
Imagine we have 1 TB of data.
Partitioning
We split the data:
              1 TB
               |
       +-------+-------+
       |               |
     Node A           Node B
    500 GB            500 GB
Node A owns one subset.
Node B owns another subset.
For example:
Node A:
users 1 - 50M

Node B:
users 50M - 100M
This is partitioning/sharding.
Each node has different data.
________________________________________
Replication
Instead:
             1 TB
              |
       +------+------+
       |      |      |
       v      v      v
      A       B      C
     1TB     1TB    1TB
Every node has a copy of the same logical dataset.
So:
Technique	Node A	Node B	Node C
Partitioning	A-data	B-data	C-data
Replication	all data	all data	all data
And production systems frequently combine them:
                 Cluster
                    |
       +------------+------------+
       |                         |
    Shard 1                   Shard 2
       |                         |
   +---+---+                 +---+---+
   |   |   |                 |   |   |
   A   B   C                 D   E   F
   R   R   R                 R   R   R
For example:
Shard 1
  Leader
  Replica
  Replica

Shard 2
  Leader
  Replica
  Replica
This distinction will become extremely important when we discuss production architectures in Lesson 6.
________________________________________
3. Problem #1: Hardware fails
This is the most fundamental reason for replication.
Suppose your database runs on:
Server A
Server A has:
•	CPU 
•	RAM 
•	SSD 
•	network interface 
•	power supply 
•	operating system 
•	database process 
You might think:
"My data is persisted to disk, so it's safe."
Not necessarily.
Imagine:
Application
     |
     v
+-----------+
| Server A  |
|           |
| Database  |
| SSD       |
+-----------+
Now the machine dies.
Maybe:
•	motherboard failure 
•	SSD failure 
•	filesystem corruption 
•	kernel crash 
•	database corruption 
•	power failure 
•	accidental shutdown 
•	cloud instance failure 
Your data may still exist somewhere in theory, but your service cannot access it.
That's the first important distinction:
Durability and availability are different properties.
________________________________________
4. Durability ≠ availability
Suppose your database writes data to an SSD.
Then:
INSERT order 123
       |
       v
    SSD
The data may survive a process restart.
That's durability.
But if the entire machine disappears:
       X
+-----------+
| Server A  |
| Database  |
+-----------+
your application cannot serve requests.
So:
Durability:
"Does the data survive?"

Availability:
"Can clients access the service?"
Replication can improve availability because another node may still possess the data.
For example:
                Application
                     |
                     v
                 Database A
                     |
              replication
                     |
                     v
                 Database B
A dies:
                Application
                     |
                     X
                 Database A
                     |
                 Database B
Now the system can potentially redirect traffic to B.
This gives us our first major motivation:
Replication allows another node to continue serving data when one node fails.
________________________________________
5. But why not just use backups?
Excellent question.
A backup is also another copy.
So why do we need replication?
Because backup and replication optimize for different goals.
Imagine:
Primary database
       |
       | every 24 hours
       v
Backup storage
At 10:00:
DB:
A B C D E
Backup:
A B C D E
At 15:00:
DB:
A B C D E F G H
Then the database dies.
Your latest backup is still:
A B C D E
You lost:
F G H
That's the Recovery Point Objective (RPO) problem.
________________________________________
With replication:
Primary
A B C D E F G H

Replica
A B C D E F G H
Potentially, the replica is much closer to the current state.
And unlike a backup, a replica can often be promoted or used to serve traffic.
So:
Backup:
  primarily recovery-oriented

Replication:
  primarily availability / scalability / locality / durability-oriented
Replication does not replace backups.
This is a very important production principle:
Replication protects you from infrastructure failure; backups protect you from data loss and logical mistakes.
And there's an even nastier problem.
________________________________________
6. Replication does not protect you from every failure
Suppose someone executes:
DELETE FROM users;
without a WHERE.
Primary:
Users = EMPTY
Replication says:
"Okay, I'll copy that change."
Replica:
Users = EMPTY
Another replica:
Users = EMPTY
Now your entire replicated cluster agrees on the wrong state.
This is why:
Replication != backup
Replication copies changes, including potentially bad changes.
Backups, point-in-time recovery, WAL archives, snapshots, etc. can give you a path to recover from logical errors.
This distinction becomes very important in real production systems.
________________________________________
