8. Problem #3: Read scalability
Now suppose the database isn't failing.
Instead, the database is simply too busy.
Suppose:
100,000 reads/sec
5,000 writes/sec
and the database has one machine.
If the workload is heavily read-oriented, adding replicas can help.
For example:
                   Application
                       |
                 +-----+-----+
                 |           |
                 v           v
              Primary      Replica 1
                 |
                 v
              Replica 2
Writes:
Application
     |
     v
Primary
Reads:
Application
     |
     +--> Replica 1
     |
     +--> Replica 2
     |
     +--> Replica 3
Now read traffic can be distributed.
This gives us another major reason:
Replication can increase read capacity by distributing reads across multiple copies.
But there's a giant caveat.
We'll eventually discover that:
Adding replicas does not automatically increase database scalability.
Why?
Because every write may still have to propagate to every replica.
If:
Primary = 10k writes/sec
and you have 10 replicas, the system now has to deal with replication traffic and replica maintenance.
Replication moves load around; it doesn't make computation disappear.
________________________________________
9. Read scaling changes the architecture
Without replicas:
             +----------+
Writes ----->|          |
Reads ------>| Database |
             |          |
             +----------+
With replicas:
                  +------------+
Writes ---------->|  Primary   |
                  +------------+
                       |
              replication stream
               /       |       \
              v        v        v
           Replica1 Replica2 Replica3
              ^        ^        ^
              |        |        |
              +--------+--------+
                   Reads
Now you've introduced a distributed system.
And distributed systems bring new questions:
What if Replica 2 is behind?
What if Replica 3 dies?
What if the primary dies?
What if a write succeeds on primary but hasn't reached Replica 1?
What if the application writes data and immediately reads from a replica?
Those questions are the heart of Lessons 2–5.
________________________________________
10. Problem #4: Geographic distribution
Now imagine your company operates globally.
Database:
Virginia
User:
Tokyo
Request:
Tokyo
  |
  | ~ long network distance
  v
Virginia DB
Every request has network latency.
You could put replicas closer to users:
                  Primary
                 Virginia
                    |
        +-----------+-----------+
        |                       |
        v                       v
   Europe Replica         Asia Replica
Now:
European users --> Europe replica
Asian users -----> Asia replica
This can improve read latency.
But now we have another trade-off:
lower read latency
       vs
replication consistency
The Asia replica might not yet have the latest write from Virginia.
That is replication lag.
Lesson 3 will be devoted to this.
