11. Problem #5: Disaster recovery
Let's make the failure more serious.
Imagine:
Primary:
US-East
and replica:
US-West
Suppose an entire region becomes unavailable.
For example:
US-East
   X X X X X
If the only database lives there:
Application ---> X
You're in serious trouble.
But with a geographically separated replica:
          US-East
        +----------+
        | Primary  |
        +----------+
              |
              |
          replication
              |
              v
          US-West
        +----------+
        | Replica  |
        +----------+
you potentially have a disaster-recovery path.
This is fundamentally different from just having two machines in the same rack.
________________________________________
12. Failure domains matter
Consider this:
       Data Center
       +---------------------+
       |                     |
       | DB A      DB B      |
       |                     |
       +---------------------+
You have replication.
But what if:
Data center power failure
Both disappear.
So:
Replication across machines
is not necessarily enough.
You need to think about failure domains.
For example:
                 Region
              /           \
          AZ-1             AZ-2
           |                 |
         DB-A              DB-B
Better:
Region
 |
 +--- AZ-1 ---> DB A
 |
 +--- AZ-2 ---> DB B
 |
 +--- AZ-3 ---> DB C
Now a single availability-zone failure doesn't necessarily destroy every replica.
For disaster recovery:
Region A
   |
   | replication
   v
Region B
The fundamental design principle is:
Copies are useful only if their failure domains are sufficiently independent.
Three replicas on the same physical host are not meaningful fault tolerance.
________________________________________
13. Problem #6: Maintenance without total downtime
Replication can also help operationally.
Suppose:
Database A
needs:
•	OS upgrade 
•	database upgrade 
•	hardware migration 
•	configuration change 
•	maintenance 
With one server:
Database
   |
maintenance
   |
