16. The key mental model: replication is a trade-off machine
Don't think:
"Replication = more reliability."
Think:
Replication gives us multiple copies, and then we must decide how those copies coordinate.
That coordination determines:
•	write latency 
•	read latency 
•	consistency 
•	availability 
•	data-loss risk 
•	recovery behavior 
•	operational complexity 
So the interesting question isn't:
"Should I use replication?"
In production, the answer is almost always yes for important distributed databases.
The interesting question is:
What replication protocol and topology give me the properties my workload needs?
________________________________________
17. A useful mathematical model
Suppose we have:
N replicas
and a failure independently affects a replica with probability:
p
If failures were truly independent, the probability of all N replicas failing simultaneously would roughly be:
p^N
For example, if:
p = 0.01
then:
1 replica:     0.01
2 replicas:    0.0001
3 replicas:    0.000001
This illustrates why multiple independent copies can dramatically improve reliability.
But don't blindly apply this equation to production.
Why?
Because failures aren't always independent.
For example:
3 DB replicas
     |
same AZ
     |
power failure
     ↓
ALL DEAD
The independence assumption collapses.
This is why failure-domain diversity is often more important than simply increasing replica count.
________________________________________
18. Replication also introduces a new failure mode
Here's the most important conceptual transition.
Before replication:
Single database

failure:
Database unavailable
After replication:
Primary
   |
replication
   |
Replica
Now we have additional failure possibilities:
Primary failure
Replica failure
Network partition
Replication process failure
Replica falling behind
Corrupted replica
Split-brain
Stale reads
Conflicting writes
Promotion failure
So replication solves some problems while creating others.
This is a recurring distributed-systems principle:
Redundancy improves resilience but increases coordination complexity.
