21. What replication does NOT automatically guarantee
This is worth memorizing conceptually.
Replication does not automatically mean:
❌ Strong consistency
Two replicas may temporarily disagree.
❌ Zero data loss
If replication is asynchronous and the primary dies before a change reaches another durable copy, that change may be lost.
❌ High availability
Poorly designed replication can still fail over badly.
❌ Read scalability
Replicas can become bottlenecks themselves.
❌ Disaster recovery
Three replicas in one failure domain don't protect against that domain disappearing.
❌ Correctness
A replicated bad write is still a bad write.
❌ Automatic failover
Replication and failover are related but different mechanisms.
This distinction will be extremely useful in interviews and architecture reviews.
________________________________________
22. Replication vs backup vs partitioning
Put these three side by side:
Mechanism	Main idea	Primary purpose
Replication	Multiple copies of same logical data	Availability, durability, read scaling, locality
Backup	Historical/preserved copy	Recovery from data loss/corruption
Partitioning	Different data on different nodes	Storage and compute scalability
Real production systems often use all three:
                 Database cluster
                       |
            +----------+----------+
            |                     |
         Shard 1                Shard 2
            |                     |
       +----+----+           +----+----+
       |    |    |           |    |    |
      P1   R1   R2          P2   R3   R4
And separately:
Database
   |
   +---- WAL/archive
   |
   +---- backups
   |
   +---- snapshots
This is much closer to how serious production systems are designed.
