________________________________________
39. A useful incident example
Imagine production alert:
Replica R2 lag > 30 sec
An inexperienced response:
"Restart R2."
A better investigation:
Primary WAL generation:
↑ 3x normal

R2 receive rate:
normal

R2 replay rate:
↓
Now we know:
network = healthy
replica receiving = healthy
replay = bottleneck
Next:
CPU = 98%
Then:
analytics query running
Root cause:
analytics workload
      ↓
CPU saturation
      ↓
replay slows
      ↓
replication lag
Solution might be:
move analytics workload
rather than:
restart database
That's the kind of reasoning I want you to develop.
________________________________________
40. The relationship between lag and synchronous replication
Remember Lesson 2.
With asynchronous replication:
Primary commit
     |
     |---- later ---->
     Replica apply
Lag can exist after the client receives success.
With synchronous replication:
Primary
   |
   v
replica durability/commit point
   |
   v
client ACK
The commit protocol constrains how far a required replica can be behind at the acknowledgement boundary.
But that doesn't mean every replica in the cluster is always caught up.
For example:
Primary
  |
  +--> synchronous R1
  |
  +--> async R2
  |
  +--> async R3
You can have:
R1: essentially current for commit guarantee
R2: 2 sec behind
R3: 30 sec behind
So synchronous replication doesn't eliminate replication lag globally.
________________________________________
41. The deepest mental model
Think of the primary as producing a stream:
L1 → L2 → L3 → L4 → L5 → L6 → L7 → ...
Every replica has a cursor:
R1 cursor = L7
R2 cursor = L6
R3 cursor = L2
So:
R1: current
R2: slightly behind
R3: significantly behind
Replication lag is fundamentally:
The distance between the authoritative history and a replica's consumed/applied position.
That definition is more powerful than "replica is slow."
________________________________________
42. Three different kinds of "behind"
This distinction is worth remembering.
Behind in transport
Primary generated X
Replica hasn't received X
Behind in durability
Replica received X
but hasn't durably persisted X
Behind in application
Replica has X in its log
but hasn't replayed/applied X
And therefore:
When troubleshooting lag, find the first stage where progress stops.
________________________________________
43. Interview-level question
Suppose an interviewer says:
"We have 3 read replicas and one has 20 seconds replication lag. What would you do?"
Don't answer:
"Increase the number of replicas."
Instead:
First
Determine whether lag is:
receive lag
flush lag
replay lag
Then
Compare:
WAL generation rate
vs
replica receive rate
vs
replica replay rate
Then investigate:
CPU
disk
network
locks
long queries
large transactions
workload spikes
Then decide:
optimize replica
reduce workload
remove replica from read pool
re-route reads
scale hardware
change topology
That's a much stronger answer.
________________________________________
44. Now connect all three lessons
We've built this:
Lesson 1
Why replicate?
availability
durability
read scaling
geography
disaster recovery
maintenance
Lesson 2
When do we acknowledge a write?
async:
ACK before replica catches up

sync:
ACK after required replication condition
Lesson 3
What happens between those points?
Primary
  |
  | generate
  v
WAL
  |
  | network
  v
Replica WAL
  |
  | replay
  v
Replica state
The gap between these stages is replication lag.
________________________________________
45. The key formulas/concepts to remember
Backlog
backlog ≈ generated work - processed work
Catch-up condition
replica processing rate > primary generation rate
If:
μ > λ
the replica can catch up.
If:
μ <= λ
the backlog cannot sustainably shrink.
Catch-up time
Approximately:
catch-up time
≈ backlog / (replica rate - generation rate)
under relatively stable conditions.
Application consequence
replication lag
      ↓
stale replica
      ↓
stale read
      ↓
possible user-visible inconsistency
Recovery consequence
replication lag
      ↓
stale failover candidate
      ↓
potential data loss
