46. What I want you to internalize
The beginner thinks:
"Replica is behind."
The experienced engineer thinks:
"Which position is it behind?"
The senior engineer thinks:
"Which stage is behind?"
The distributed-systems engineer thinks:
"What workload/resource constraint caused that stage to fall behind, what freshness guarantee does the application require, and what happens to RPO and failover safety if the lag persists?"
That's the progression we're aiming for.

Lesson 4 — Failure and Recovery
This is the lesson where replication becomes a real distributed-systems problem.
So far:
•	Lesson 1: Why have multiple copies? 
•	Lesson 2: When can we acknowledge a write? 
•	Lesson 3: How far behind can replicas be? 
Now:
What happens when the primary disappears?
And more importantly:
How do we safely choose a new primary without losing data or accidentally having two primaries?
That question leads us into:
•	failure detection 
•	failover 
•	leader election 
•	quorum 
•	split-brain 
•	fencing 
•	promotion 
•	RPO 
•	recovery 
•	re-synchronization 
•	old-primary rejoining 
________________________________________
1. Start with the normal system
Suppose we have:
                    Application
                         |
                         v
                    +---------+
                    | Primary |
                    +---------+
                     /   |   \
                    /    |    \
                   v     v     v
                  R1     R2     R3
Assume:
R1 = 10 ms behind
R2 = 20 ms behind
R3 = 2 seconds behind
Everything is healthy.
The primary accepts writes:
W100
W101
W102
W103
and replicates them.
Now:
                    X
                    |
                +---------+
                | Primary |
                +---------+
The primary disappears.
The application still needs somewhere to send writes.
So the system needs:
failure detection
       ↓
choose candidate
       ↓
promote candidate
       ↓
redirect traffic
       ↓
old primary must not interfere
That sequence is failover.
________________________________________
2. First distinction: failure vs failover
These are not the same.
Failure
Something has gone wrong:
Primary crashed
Failover
The system responds:
Primary failed
      ↓
choose another node
      ↓
promote it
      ↓
route clients there
You can have:
failure without automatic failover
For example, an engineer manually promotes a replica.
Or:
automatic failover
where an orchestration system performs the transition.
________________________________________
3. Failure detection is surprisingly difficult
Suppose:
Primary
   |
   X
   |
Replica
Replica can't reach primary.
What does that mean?
Possibility 1:
Primary crashed
Possibility 2:
Network between them failed
Possibility 3:
Replica's network failed
Possibility 4:
Primary is overloaded and not responding
Possibility 5:
Firewall/routing problem
The replica cannot directly observe reality.
It only observes:
"I cannot communicate with the primary."
This is a foundational distributed-systems problem.
________________________________________
4. Failure detection is based on suspicion
Imagine a health check:
Replica → Primary: "Are you alive?"
No response.
After 1 second:
retry
No response.
After 5 seconds:
retry
No response.
Eventually:
"Primary is probably dead."
Notice the word:
probably
A distributed node usually cannot know with absolute certainty that another node has failed.
It can only make a decision based on timeouts, heartbeats, network observations, and protocol state.
________________________________________
5. Why aggressive failure detection is dangerous
Suppose the primary is healthy but temporarily slow.
Primary
CPU = 100%
Health-check response takes:
8 seconds
But your failure detector says:
timeout = 5 sec
The system concludes:
Primary is dead!
and promotes R1.
Now:
Old Primary: still running
New Primary: R1
You potentially have two writers.
That's the beginning of split-brain.
________________________________________
6. Split-brain
This is one of the most dangerous failure scenarios.
Imagine:
              Network partition
                    X
             +------+------+
             |             |
             v             v
          Primary          R1
The old primary thinks:
"I am still primary."
R1 thinks:
"The primary is dead. I am primary."
Now:
Client A ---> Old Primary ---> W1
Client B ---> R1 -----------> W2
We have:
             TWO WRITERS
Potentially:
Old Primary:
balance = 900

R1:
balance = 800
Both are accepting legitimate writes.
When the network heals:
Old Primary <----------> R1
What happens?
Which version wins?
This is not ordinary replication lag.
You've created divergent histories.
________________________________________
7. Why split-brain is worse than a failed primary
If the primary simply dies:
Primary X
you have one missing node.
If two nodes both believe they're primary:
Primary A
Primary B
you now have conflicting authority.
A failed node can often be replaced.
Two independent writers can corrupt the logical history.
Therefore a core goal of failover systems is:
Never allow an old primary and a new primary to simultaneously act as authoritative writers.
That leads to fencing.
________________________________________
8. Fencing
Fencing means:
Prevent the old primary from continuing to perform writes after leadership has moved elsewhere.
There are multiple ways to do this.
Conceptually:
Old Primary
     |
     X
  fenced
while:
New Primary
     |
     v
accept writes
The important thing is that the old node must not merely be asked to stop.
The system needs a mechanism that makes continued writes impossible or invalid.
________________________________________
9. Fencing example: power control
Imagine physical servers.
A management system can remotely power off the old primary:
Controller
    |
    | power off
    v
Old Primary
    X
Now the new primary can safely take over.
This is sometimes called STONITH:
Shoot The Other Node In The Head.
The terminology sounds dramatic, but the principle is simple:
guaranteed isolation of old leader
________________________________________
10. Fencing doesn't have to mean power-off
Other mechanisms can include:
Storage fencing
Old primary loses access to the storage.
Network fencing
Old primary is isolated from clients/database network.
Lease expiration
Old primary's leadership lease expires.
Epoch/term validation
Writes from an old leader are rejected because its leadership term is stale.
For example:
Old Primary:
term = 10

New Primary:
term = 11
Any operation carrying:
term = 10
is rejected.
This is a very powerful pattern.
________________________________________
11. Epochs/terms
Imagine leadership generations:
Term 1:
Primary = A

Term 2:
Primary = B

Term 3:
Primary = C
Every operation is associated with a term.
So:
A says:
"I am primary, term 1."
But the cluster knows:
Current term = 2
Therefore:
A's write → REJECT
This protects against an old primary returning from a network partition and attempting to continue writing.
This idea appears in many consensus/replication systems.
________________________________________
12. Now let's talk about choosing the new primary
Suppose:
Primary X

R1 = caught up
R2 = 100 ms behind
R3 = 30 sec behind
Primary dies.
We could simply choose:
R1
because it is freshest.
But real systems need more than freshness.
They also need to reason about:
•	which nodes are reachable 
•	which nodes have durable state 
•	quorum 
•	leadership terms 
•	failure domains 
•	whether a node is allowed to become leader 
•	whether it has all committed transactions 
•	whether another node may still be leader 
So failover is not:
SELECT replica ORDER BY lag LIMIT 1
It's a distributed coordination problem.
________________________________________
13. RPO becomes extremely important
Remember:
RPO = Recovery Point Objective
Suppose:
Primary:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W6

Replica:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5
Primary crashes.
If replica becomes primary:
W6
may be lost.
So:
RPO = potentially 1 or more writes
With asynchronous replication, the RPO depends on how far the surviving replica was behind.
________________________________________
14. Timeline of asynchronous failure
Let's make this precise.
Time
---------------------------------------------------->

Primary:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W6 W7
                     X crash

Replica:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5
At the moment of failure:
Primary history = W1...W7
Replica history = W1...W5
After promotion:
New primary:
W1...W5
Potentially lost:
W6 W7
So replication lag directly influences recovery-point loss.
This connects Lessons 2, 3, and 4:
async replication
       ↓
possible lag
       ↓
primary failure
       ↓
stale replica promoted
       ↓
possible data loss
________________________________________
15. What about synchronous replication?
Suppose commit requires:
Primary + R1 durable
Then:
Primary:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5

R1:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5
Primary crashes.
R1 contains the acknowledged transactions.
So under the intended protocol guarantees:
RPO for acknowledged writes ≈ 0
assuming the synchronous durability guarantee actually covers durable independent storage.
This is why synchronous replication can be valuable for critical workloads.
________________________________________
16. But synchronous replication doesn't mean "zero data loss under every imaginable disaster"
Important nuance.
Suppose:
Primary
   |
   +--> synchronous replica
Both are in the same physical building.
A catastrophic event destroys:
both
Now:
RPO > 0
unless you have another durable copy elsewhere.
Therefore:
Zero data loss requires thinking about the failure domain, not just synchronous acknowledgement.
You might use:
Region A
Primary + synchronous standby

Region B
asynchronous disaster-recovery replica
Now you're balancing:
•	local durability 
•	write latency 
•	regional disaster recovery 
________________________________________
17. Quorum and failure
Let's use three nodes:
A
B
C
Suppose the system requires:
2/3 quorum
Normal state:
A = leader
B = follower
C = follower
Now A dies:
A X

B
C
B and C still have:
2/3
So they can potentially establish a new leader.
This is powerful because one node can fail without making the cluster incapable of making progress.
________________________________________
18. Why majority matters
Suppose instead:
3 nodes
and two separate groups form:
Partition 1:
A + B

Partition 2:
C
Only A+B has a majority.
Therefore:
A+B → can establish authority
C   → cannot
This prevents both partitions from independently becoming leaders.
This is one of the deepest reasons majority/quorum protocols are useful:
Two disjoint majorities cannot exist simultaneously.
For a cluster of 3:
majority = 2
Two groups cannot both contain 2 nodes without sharing a node.
That shared-node property helps prevent conflicting leadership.
________________________________________
19. Five-node cluster
For:
A B C D E
majority:
3
Possible:
A B C
has quorum.
Remaining:
D E
doesn't.
But:
A B
doesn't have quorum.
Therefore it can't safely establish new authority by itself.
This is why distributed databases and consensus systems frequently use odd numbers of voting members.
________________________________________
20. Why not 4 nodes?
Four nodes:
A B C D
majority:
3
You can tolerate only one failure.
Three nodes:
A B C
majority:
2
also tolerates one failure.
So:
3 nodes → tolerate 1
4 nodes → tolerate 1
The fourth node may provide additional capacity/replica storage, but doesn't improve majority failure tolerance.
For voting purposes:
3 → 5 → 7
is often more meaningful than:
3 → 4 → 5
This is a quorum concept, not a universal database rule.
________________________________________
21. Quorum isn't the same as replication factor
This distinction matters.
Suppose:
replication factor = 5
meaning five copies exist.
But the write quorum might be:
3
So:
5 copies
3 required acknowledgements
And read quorum could be:
3
depending on the system.
These are different parameters:
Replication factor
    =
how many copies exist

Write quorum
    =
how many participants must acknowledge a write

Read quorum
    =
how many participants participate in a read
We'll revisit this when discussing read/write consistency.
________________________________________
22. Failover sequence
Let's construct a realistic conceptual failover.
Normal:
             Client
                |
                v
             Primary
             /     \
            v       v
           R1       R2
Primary fails.
Step 1 — Failure detection
Primary
