become follower
That last part is extremely important.
________________________________________
23. The zombie-primary problem
Imagine:
Old Primary A
     X
network partition
R1 becomes primary.
Later A comes back.
A still thinks:
"I was primary."
If A begins accepting writes:
A → W100
R1 → W101
we're back to split-brain.
Therefore:
A recovered node must prove that it is no longer authoritative before it can re-enter the cluster.
This is where:
•	terms 
•	epochs 
•	fencing 
•	leader leases 
•	cluster metadata 
become essential.
________________________________________
24. Why the old primary cannot simply continue from its local state
Suppose:
Old Primary A:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W6

New Primary B:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W7 W8
A and B have diverged.
A cannot simply say:
"I'll send W6 to B."
Because B has already established a different history.
The system needs a controlled re-synchronization procedure.
This can involve:
discarding divergent suffix
+
copying missing state
+
replaying logs
The exact mechanism depends on the database.
________________________________________
25. Think of database history as a log
This mental model makes recovery easier.
Imagine:
         Log
          |
          v
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W6 W7 W8
Primary A had:
W1...W8
New primary B had:
W1...W5
and then continued with:
W6' W7'
Now histories diverged:
A:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W6 W7 W8

B:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5 W6' W7'
A cannot simply rejoin with its old suffix.
It needs to discard:
W6 W7 W8
and synchronize with B's authoritative history.
This is why recovery is fundamentally a history reconciliation problem.
________________________________________
26. Failover isn't free
During failover:
Primary failure
      ↓
detection
      ↓
election
      ↓
promotion
      ↓
routing update
there may be a period where:
writes unavailable
This is failover downtime.
For example:
T0 = primary dies
T1 = detected
T2 = election
T3 = promotion
T4 = traffic redirected
Then:
failover duration = T4 - T0
If that's:
5 seconds
your system may be highly available but not literally zero-downtime.
________________________________________
27. Availability is a probability, not magic
Suppose:
MTBF = mean time between failures
MTTR = mean time to recovery
A simple availability approximation is:
Availability ≈ MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR)
For example:
MTBF = 1000 hours
MTTR = 1 hour
Then:
≈ 1000 / 1001
≈ 99.90%
Reducing recovery time can therefore significantly improve availability.
Replication + automatic failover primarily helps reduce:
MTTR
________________________________________
28. But automatic failover can make things worse
Suppose the primary has a temporary network problem.
Automatic system says:
Primary dead!
and promotes R1.
But primary is actually alive.
Now:
A = old primary
B = new primary
If fencing fails:
A accepts writes
B accepts writes
You traded:
temporary outage
for:
data corruption/divergence
Therefore:
Fast failover is not automatically better failover.
The system needs a safe failure detector and authority transition.
________________________________________
29. False positives vs false negatives
Failure detection has two broad errors.
False positive
System thinks a healthy node is dead.
healthy primary
      ↓
false suspicion
      ↓
unnecessary failover
Danger:
split-brain
________________________________________
False negative
System doesn't detect a dead node quickly enough.
primary dead
      ↓
slow detection
      ↓
longer outage
Danger:
availability loss
So failure detectors have a trade-off:
aggressive detection
    ↓
faster recovery
    +
more false positives

conservative detection
    ↓
fewer false positives
    +
longer outage
This is a general distributed-systems trade-off.
________________________________________
30. Network partition is the hardest case
Consider:
        Network
           X
      +----+----+
      |         |
      v         v
    Node A    Node B
Both nodes are healthy.
They simply cannot communicate.
If both accept writes:
A → W1 W2 W3

B → W4 W5 W6
you have two histories.
A safe distributed database needs a rule for who has authority.
Typically:
majority side
can continue.
Minority side:
stop writes
This is one reason quorum-based systems sacrifice some availability during partitions.
________________________________________
31. Why the minority side often must stop
Suppose 3 nodes:
A B C
Partition:
A B | C
A+B has majority.
C has minority.
If C is allowed to elect itself leader:
A+B → leader A
C   → leader C
Now you have two leaders.
Instead:
A+B → continue
C   → no leadership
This gives us:
Availability for the majority partition, safety for the cluster as a whole.
________________________________________
32. What happens to in-flight writes?
This is another subtle failure scenario.
Client sends:
WRITE W100
Primary begins processing.
Then primary crashes.
Client receives:
??? 
Maybe:
•	timeout 
•	connection reset 
•	no response 
The client doesn't know:
Did W100 commit?
This is called an ambiguous outcome.
The application may retry:
WRITE W100
Now you need idempotency.
________________________________________
33. Why distributed failures create "unknown outcomes"
Suppose:
Client → Primary:
"Commit W100"

Primary:
commits W100

Primary:
crashes before sending ACK
Client sees:
timeout
Client thinks:
"Maybe it failed."
But database state is:
W100 = committed
If client retries blindly:
W100 again
you could duplicate an operation.
This is why robust systems use:
•	transaction IDs 
•	idempotency keys 
•	unique constraints 
•	request deduplication 
especially for operations like payments/orders.
________________________________________
34. Java example: retry danger
Imagine:
paymentService.charge(customer, 100);
The server processes:
charge = SUCCESS
but network connection dies before the Java service gets the response.
Your service sees:
TimeoutException
and retries:
paymentService.charge(customer, 100);
Potential result:
charge $100
charge $100
This is why:
distributed failure
+
retry
requires idempotency.
For example:
idempotency_key = "payment-12345"
The database can enforce:
same key → same logical operation
This is an application-level complement to database recovery.
________________________________________
35. Recovery has two different meanings
Be careful with the word recovery.
Crash recovery
Database process/machine restarts and uses its log to recover committed state.
Example:
Database crashes
    ↓
restart
    ↓
read WAL
    ↓
redo/undo as needed
    ↓
consistent local state
Cluster recovery
A failed primary is replaced by another node.
Example:
Primary dies
    ↓
replica promoted
    ↓
traffic redirected
These are related but different.
________________________________________
36. Crash recovery vs failover
Think:
Crash recovery:
"Can this node reconstruct its own state?"

Failover:
"Can another node safely take authority?"
A database can successfully recover its own disk state while the application still experiences downtime.
Conversely, a replica can be promoted quickly while the old primary still requires later crash recovery/reconciliation.
________________________________________
37. Redo and undo intuition
At the storage engine level, databases often use logging mechanisms to recover from crashes.
Conceptually:
Transaction
    |
    v
WAL/log
    |
    v
data pages
If the process crashes after:
log = committed
data page = not fully written
recovery can replay the log.
So:
WAL
is not merely a replication mechanism.
It is also central to local crash recovery.
This is why WAL-based databases can recover from process/machine crashes without needing a remote replica for every crash scenario.
________________________________________
38. Recovery after promotion
Suppose R1 becomes primary.
Now:
             New Primary R1
                  |
             +----+----+
             |         |
             v         v
            R2        R3
What about the old primary?
Eventually:
Old Primary A
returns.
It cannot simply become:
R4
without synchronization.
It may have:
old history
that differs from:
new authoritative history
So recovery often looks like:
Old A
 |
 | discard/reconcile stale state
 v
synchronize from R1
 |
 v
healthy replica
________________________________________
39. Full resynchronization vs incremental catch-up
Suppose old primary is only slightly behind.
You might be able to do:
R1
 |
 | missing WAL
 v
Old A
and catch up incrementally.
But suppose:
Old A has diverged badly
or its required log history is no longer available.
Then you may need:
full snapshot/base backup
        +
new WAL
Conceptually:
R1
 |
 +--> snapshot
 |
 +--> subsequent log
 |
 v
Old A
This can be expensive for very large databases.
________________________________________
40. Why recovery capacity matters
Imagine a:
10 TB database
and a replica is completely lost.
If rebuilding it requires:
10 TB transfer
your recovery time could be hours.
Therefore:
A system's recovery architecture must be designed with database size and network/storage throughput in mind.
Having "3 replicas" doesn't automatically mean fast recovery.
________________________________________
41. Failure domains again
Suppose:
AZ-1:
Primary
Replica 1

AZ-2:
Replica 2
AZ-1 fails:
Primary X
Replica 1 X
Replica 2 survives
You can promote Replica 2.
But now the cluster has only:
one healthy copy
If you don't rebuild another replica quickly:
Replica 2
    |
    X
future failure
could cause another outage.
So after failover, you need to think about:
Restoring redundancy, not merely restoring service.
________________________________________
42. Recovery has phases
A mature way to think about recovery is:
1. Detect failure
       ↓
2. Establish authority
       ↓
3. Promote new leader
       ↓
4. Restore client traffic
       ↓
5. Rebuild lost redundancy
       ↓
6. Rejoin recovered nodes
       ↓
7. Verify replication health
Notice:
Service recovery and redundancy recovery are separate milestones.
You may restore traffic in 10 seconds but need 30 minutes to rebuild the missing replica.
________________________________________
43. This affects your SLO
Suppose your service has:
SLO:
99.99% availability
You need to care about:
failure frequency
+
failover time
+
recovery time
+
re-replication time
Not merely:
"we have replicas."
