47. Lease-based leadership
One common conceptual approach is:
Leader A
holds lease until T
As long as A's lease is valid:
A can write
After expiry:
A cannot safely assume leadership
A new leader can obtain a newer lease.
The tricky part is ensuring time assumptions and storage/coordination semantics are strong enough.
This is why production distributed systems are very careful about clocks, leases, and fencing.
________________________________________
48. Why clocks are dangerous
Imagine A thinks:
lease expires at 12:00:10
but A's clock is wrong.
B thinks:
it's already 12:00:11
A thinks:
it's 12:00:09
Now both might think they're valid.
Therefore:
Time-based mechanisms require carefully engineered clock/lease semantics.
Distributed systems don't casually assume:

________________________________________
49. Consensus enters the picture
At this point you may notice:
"We're talking about leader election, quorum, terms, and preventing two leaders."
Exactly.
That's the territory of consensus protocols such as Raft/Paxos-style designs.
You don't need every database to literally implement textbook Raft in its storage engine, but conceptually the system needs to solve questions like:
Who is leader?
Which writes are committed?
Which history is authoritative?
Can a new leader safely take over?
How do nodes agree after partitions?
This is why:
Replication and consensus are related but not identical.
________________________________________
50. Replication vs consensus
Replication:
Keep multiple copies of data.
Consensus:
Get distributed nodes to agree on an authoritative sequence/decision despite failures.
You can have:
simple asynchronous primary-replica replication
without strong consensus.
But robust automatic leader election and failover often require some form of distributed coordination/consensus.
This distinction is extremely useful for system design.
________________________________________
51. A common misconception: "The load balancer handles failover"
Suppose:
Load Balancer
    |
    +--> Primary
    +--> Replica
The load balancer knows:
TCP connection works
But does it know:
Which database has authoritative state?
Which node is leader?
Which node has the latest committed transaction?
Is this replica safe to promote?
Not necessarily.
Database failover is a stateful coordination problem, not merely health checking.
A TCP health check is insufficient.
________________________________________
52. Another misconception: "Just use the freshest replica"
Suppose:
R1 = 10 ms behind
R2 = 20 ms behind
R1 looks better.
But suppose:
R1 is in isolated partition
R2 has quorum connectivity
R1 may be fresher but unable to establish legitimate leadership.
Therefore:
Freshness is necessary but not sufficient for safe promotion.
The candidate must also have legitimate authority under the cluster's protocol.
________________________________________
53. Another misconception: "Failover means zero data loss"
No.
If replication is asynchronous:
Primary:
W1 W2 W3 W4 W5

Replica:
W1 W2 W3
Failover may lose:
W4 W5
So:
Failover ≠ zero RPO
Zero-loss failover requires stronger commit/durability semantics.
________________________________________
54. Another misconception: "More replicas always improve recovery"
Suppose:
5 replicas
but all are:
same AZ
same storage backend
same network
A shared infrastructure failure can remove all five.
Or suppose replicas are extremely slow to rebuild.
Quantity alone isn't sufficient.
You need:
independence
+
correct placement
+
safe failover protocol
+
recovery capacity
________________________________________
55. Recovery and RTO
We've discussed RPO.
Now:
RTO = Recovery Time Objective
Informally:
How long can the service be unavailable?
Suppose:
RPO = 0
RTO = 30 seconds
You need:
•	sufficiently strong replication 
•	automatic failure detection 
•	fast leader election 
•	fast routing change 
•	sufficiently warm replicas 
Compare:
RPO = 1 minute
RTO = 30 minutes
A much cheaper architecture may be acceptable.
Therefore replication architecture should be driven by:
RTO
as well as:
consistency
throughput
________________________________________
56. RPO + RTO together
Think of them as:
                 Failure
                    |
        +-----------+-----------+
        |                       |
       RPO                     RTO
        |                       |
 "How much data?"        "How much downtime?"
        |                       |
