74. One final example tying everything together
Suppose:
Primary P
Replica R1
Replica R2
Initial:
balance = $100
User withdraws:
$30
Primary commits:
balance = $70
But async replication:
P  = $70
R1 = $70
R2 = $100
Read from R2
$100
That's a stale read.
________________________________________
Now suppose the user refreshes again and gets R1:
$70
Then load balancer sends the next request to R2:
$100
Now the user observed:
$70 → $100
That's a monotonic-read violation.
________________________________________
Now suppose the user writes:
withdraw $30
and immediately reads from R2:
$100
That's a read-your-writes violation.
________________________________________
Now suppose P crashes before R1 receives the withdrawal:
P X
R1 = $100
R2 = $100
R1 becomes primary.
The successful withdrawal may disappear.
That's a replication/failover RPO problem.
________________________________________
If instead the withdrawal was synchronously committed to R1:
P = $70
R1 = $70
R2 = $100
P dies.
R1 becomes primary:
balance = $70
The acknowledged write survives.
That's the connection between:
write acknowledgement
       ↓
replication
       ↓
consistency
       ↓
failure recovery
