76. The five questions I would expect you to answer in an interview
Given:
Primary P
R1
R2
and asynchronous replication, I would ask:
Q1
A write returned success. Can the next read return the old value?
Yes, if the read goes to a lagging replica and the system doesn't provide read-your-writes.
Q2
Can two consecutive reads return newer then older data?
Yes, if requests are routed to replicas at different replication positions and monotonic reads aren't guaranteed.
Q3
What prevents an old primary from writing after failover?
Fencing, epochs/terms, leases, quorum/consensus mechanisms, or equivalent authority controls.
Q4
Can a successful write disappear after primary failure?
Yes under asynchronous replication if the acknowledged write hadn't reached the surviving authoritative replica.
Q5
How would you guarantee read-your-writes while still using replicas?
Use primary/session stickiness, version/LSN tokens, wait-for-replica-catch-up, or a database-specific consistency mechanism.
If those five answers are natural to you, you've understood the core of this lesson.
________________________________________
The one sentence to remember
Replication determines where copies exist; consistency determines what a client is allowed to observe about those copies.
And that distinction is fundamental.

Lesson 6 — Production Replication Architectures
This is the lesson where the previous five lessons finally come together.
You've learned:
1.	Why replication exists 
2.	Synchronous vs asynchronous replication 
3.	Replication lag 
4.	Failure and recovery 
5.	Read/write consistency 
Now the question becomes:
How do we actually assemble these ideas into production database architectures?
A production architecture is not just:
Primary → Replica
The real questions are:
•	Where are replicas located? 
•	Who accepts writes? 
•	Who serves reads? 
•	How are replicas synchronized? 
•	What happens when the primary dies? 
•	What happens when a whole AZ dies? 
•	What happens when a region dies? 
•	Can multiple nodes accept writes? 
•	How are conflicts resolved? 
•	What consistency does each read get? 
•	How does failover happen? 
•	How do we prevent split brain? 
•	What is the RPO/RTO? 
•	How does the Java application discover topology changes? 
We'll build these architectures from simplest → most sophisticated.
________________________________________
1. Start with the canonical production architecture
The most common starting point is:
                  Application
                      |
                  Load Balancer
                      |
              +-------+-------+
              |               |
            READ            WRITE
              |               |
              v               v
         Read Replicas      Primary
          /       \         /     \
         v         v       v       v
        R1        R2      WAL/binlog/replication
Conceptually:
                  +------ R1
                  |
Client → Primary -+------ R2
                  |
                  +------ R3
Primary handles writes.
Replicas handle reads.
This is single-leader replication.
It is probably the most important production architecture to understand first.
________________________________________
2. Why single-leader replication is so popular
Because it simplifies writes.
Suppose:
orders
inventory
payments
all have updates.
If any node can accept writes, you immediately have to answer:
What happens when two nodes modify the same data concurrently?
With one leader:
              WRITE
                |
                v
             Primary
                |
        +-------+-------+
        |       |       |
        v       v       v
       R1      R2      R3
All writes have one authoritative ordering point.
That's enormously useful.
The primary can establish:
W1
W2
W3
W4
instead of having:
Node A:
W1 W4

Node B:
W2

Node C:
W3
and then figuring out how those operations relate.
________________________________________
3. Write path
Suppose a Java application executes:
orderRepository.save(order);
Conceptually:
Application
    |
    | WRITE
    v
Primary
    |
    +--> durable log
    |
    +--> Replica 1
    |
    +--> Replica 2
Depending on configuration, the primary may acknowledge:
Async
Primary durable
      ↓
ACK
      ↓
replicas catch up
or:
Sync/quorum
Primary
   |
   +--> Replica 1
   |
   +--> Replica 2
          ↓
      replication condition satisfied
          ↓
         ACK
The architecture is the same at a high level, but the acknowledgement semantics are different.
________________________________________
4. Read path
Now suppose:
GET /products
The application can route:
              +--> R1
              |
Client → Router
              |
              +--> R2
              |
              +--> R3
This allows:
10K writes/sec → Primary
100K reads/sec → Replicas
This is one of the biggest reasons replication exists.
Instead of scaling the write node vertically forever:
CPU ↑
RAM ↑
IOPS ↑
you can scale reads horizontally:
R1
R2
R3
R4
R5
...
________________________________________
5. But this immediately creates a consistency problem
Suppose:
WRITE order #123
       ↓
Primary
       ↓
success
Then:
GET order #123
       ↓
R3
but R3 is behind.
Result:
404 Not Found
even though the order was successfully created.
Therefore:
Read scaling creates consistency-routing problems.
This is why Lesson 5 was necessary before Lesson 6.
