6. Production architecture #1: Primary + Read Replicas
The simplest production architecture:
                  Application
                      |
                Connection Router
                  /         \
             writes        reads
                |             |
                v             v
             Primary       R1 R2 R3
                |
                +------------+
                |            |
                v            v
               R1           R2
Strengths
•	Simple mental model 
•	Easy write ordering 
•	Excellent read scaling 
•	Straightforward failover concept 
•	Easy to operate compared with multi-leader 
Weaknesses
•	Primary is a write bottleneck 
•	Primary failure requires failover 
•	Replication lag 
•	Reads can be stale 
•	Single-leader topology can become geographically awkward 
________________________________________
7. Production architecture #2: Primary + Standby
This architecture is different from having many read replicas.
             Primary
                |
                | replication
                v
             Standby
The standby may not serve normal application traffic.
Its purpose is:
Be ready to become the primary.
Normal state:
Primary → Standby
   ↑
writes
If Primary dies:
Primary X

Standby
   ↓
PROMOTE
   ↓
New Primary
This is primarily a high-availability architecture.
________________________________________
8. Read replica vs standby
These terms are sometimes blurred, but architecturally think of them differently.
Read replica
Purpose:
scale reads
Standby
Purpose:
survive primary failure
A single node can potentially serve both purposes, but the design intent differs.
For example:
Primary
   |
   +--> Read Replica
   |
   +--> Standby
Now you have separated:
read scaling
from:
failure recovery
This can be a very sensible production design.
________________________________________
9. Production architecture #3: Multi-AZ
Now we introduce physical failure domains.
Suppose one data center/AZ fails.
Don't put:
Primary
Replica
in the same failure domain.
Instead:
             Region
       +------------------+
       |                  |
       | AZ-A             |
       | Primary          |
       |                  |
       +------------------+
               |
               | replication
               |
       +------------------+
       | AZ-B             |
       | Standby          |
       |                  |
       +------------------+
Now:
AZ-A fails
    ↓
AZ-B survives
    ↓
promote standby
This protects against:
•	host failure 
•	rack failure 
•	power failure 
•	networking problems 
•	AZ-level outages 
depending on the provider's failure model.
________________________________________
10. Why "three replicas" isn't enough information
Suppose someone tells you:
"We have three database replicas."
That tells you almost nothing.
You need to ask:
Where?
Possibilities:
Same machine:
P R1 R2
Terrible.
Or:
same rack:
Rack A:
P R1 R2
Better, but still vulnerable.
Or:
AZ-A:
P

AZ-B:
R1

AZ-C:
R2
Much better fault isolation.
Therefore:
Replication topology must be understood together with failure domains.
________________________________________
11. Multi-AZ architecture
A common design:
                    Region
        +-----------------------------+
        |                             |
        | AZ-A          AZ-B          AZ-C
        |       +       +             |
        |       |       |             |
        |     Primary   Standby       Replica
        |       |       |             |
        +-------+-------+-------------+
Write path:
Application
     |
     v
Primary
Replication:
Primary → Standby
Primary → Replica
Read path:
Application
     |
     +--> Primary
     |
     +--> Replica
Failover:
Primary X
   ↓
Standby promoted
   ↓
new primary
________________________________________
12. Synchronous standby vs asynchronous standby
This is where Lesson 2 comes back.
Async standby
Primary
   |
   | async
   v
Standby
Potential failure:
Primary commits W100
Primary dies
W100 hasn't reached standby
Possible RPO:
> 0
Synchronous
Primary
   |
   | sync
   v
Standby
Commit requires the standby to acknowledge the required condition.
Then:
Primary dies
   ↓
Standby has committed state
Potentially:
RPO ≈ 0
for the failure model covered by that synchronous guarantee.
But latency increases.
________________________________________
13. Production architecture #4: Three-node synchronous cluster
A more robust design might be:
             +------- Node A
             |
Client → Leader
             |
             +------- Node B
             |
             +------- Node C
Suppose:
N = 3
and a majority is required:
majority = 2
Then the system can tolerate:
1 node failure
while maintaining a majority.
This leads us toward consensus-backed replication.
________________________________________
14. Consensus changes the mental model
With simple primary/replica replication:
Primary decides
Replica follows
But who decides if the primary disappears?
A consensus system introduces a protocol where nodes agree on:
Who is leader?
and:
What entries are committed?
Conceptually:
        Node A
       /      \
      /        \
 Node B ------ Node C
A leader is elected.
For example:
Term 7:
Leader = A
A fails.
Then:
Term 8:
Leader = B
The system uses the protocol to prevent multiple nodes from legitimately believing they are leader.
This is a major step up from basic asynchronous replication.
________________________________________
15. Why leader election matters
Imagine:
Old Primary A
     X
     |
network partition
A doesn't know it's disconnected.
Meanwhile:
B + C
elect B as the new primary.
Now:
A thinks:
"I am primary."

B thinks:
"I am primary."
That's split brain.
If both accept writes:
A → W101
B → W102
you have conflicting histories.
Production systems therefore need mechanisms such as:
epochs
terms
leases
quorum
fencing
consensus
to establish authority.
________________________________________
16. Fencing
Fencing is one of the most important HA concepts.
Suppose old primary A becomes isolated.
New primary B is elected.
You want:
A = no longer authorized to write
B = authorized
Even if A comes back and says:
"I'm still primary!"
the system must reject it.
Conceptually:
Epoch 10:
A is leader

A dies

Epoch 11:
B is leader

A tries write with epoch 10
       ↓
REJECT
This prevents stale leaders from corrupting state.
________________________________________
17. Production architecture #5: Multi-region primary + replicas
Now suppose you need:
•	disaster recovery 
•	global reads 
•	regional latency reduction 
Architecture:
                    Region US
                  +-----------+
                  | Primary   |
                  +-----------+
                       |
                       |
                 replication
                       |
          +------------+------------+
          |                         |
          v                         v
   Region Europe              Region India
   +-----------+              +-----------+
   | Replica   |              | Replica   |
   +-----------+              +-----------+
Users can read locally:
