21. Production architecture #6: Multi-primary / Multi-leader
Now we remove the single-write leader.
Region A Primary
       ↕
   replication
       ↕
Region B Primary
Both accept writes.
For example:
User in India → India Primary
User in Europe → Europe Primary
This is attractive for:
•	geo-distributed writes 
•	low write latency 
•	regional autonomy 
•	resilience 
But now we have the hardest problem:
Conflicts.
________________________________________
22. Multi-leader conflict example
Initial:
balance = 100
Region A:
withdraw 80
Region B simultaneously:
withdraw 50
Both see:
100
Both accept.
Then replication occurs.
What should the final state be?
If using naive last-write-wins:
20
or:
50
But logically:
100 - 80 - 50 = -30
which may violate:
balance >= 0
This is why multi-leader is substantially harder.
________________________________________
23. Conflict resolution strategies
Multi-leader systems need one or more of:
last-write-wins
version vectors
logical clocks
application-defined merge
CRDTs
operation-based replication
transaction conflict detection
The correct choice depends on the data model.
For financial state:
"just take the latest value"
is often unacceptable.
________________________________________
24. Multi-leader works better for some data
Consider:
user preferences
Maybe:
Region A:
theme = dark

Region B:
language = Telugu
These changes affect different fields.
A merge can potentially produce:
theme = dark
language = Telugu
This is much easier than:
balance
inventory
sequence number
where concurrent updates have stronger invariants.
Therefore:
Multi-leader suitability depends heavily on conflict semantics.
________________________________________
25. Production architecture #7: Leaderless replication
Now we remove the leader entirely.
Conceptually:
           +--- Node A
           |
Client ----+--- Node B
           |
           +--- Node C
A write may be sent to multiple replicas.
A read may query multiple replicas.
For example:
N = 3
W = 2
R = 2
Then:
write → 2 nodes
read  → 2 nodes
This is the quorum model from Lesson 5.
________________________________________
26. Why would you want leaderless replication?
Potential benefits:
•	no single leader 
•	high availability 
•	decentralized topology 
•	flexible failure handling 
•	good write availability in some designs 
But now the database needs mechanisms for:
version comparison
conflict detection
read repair
anti-entropy
repair/reconciliation
The complexity moves from:
leader election
toward:
conflict resolution
________________________________________
27. Read repair
Suppose:
A = version 10
B = version 10
C = version 8
A quorum read contacts:
A + C
It discovers:
version 10
version 8
The system can determine:
version 10 is newer
and repair C:
C:
8 → 10
That's read repair conceptually.
________________________________________
28. Anti-entropy
But what if C is never read?
Then read repair won't fix it.
So systems can perform background reconciliation:
A ↔ B
B ↔ C
C ↔ A
or exchange summaries of their state.
This is often called:
anti-entropy
The goal:
eventually:
A = B = C
assuming updates stop and the system remains able to communicate.
This is eventual convergence implemented as an operational mechanism.
________________________________________
29. Hinted handoff
Another distributed-database technique:
Suppose:
N = 3
and node C is temporarily unavailable.
A write intended for:
A + B + C
may be stored temporarily elsewhere:
A
B
D ← temporary handoff
When C returns:
D → C
and C catches up.
The broader lesson:
Temporary node failure doesn't necessarily mean writes must stop.
Different systems implement this differently.
________________________________________
30. Production architecture #8: Chain / hierarchical replication
Instead of:
Primary
 | | |
 R1 R2 R3
you can have:
Primary
   |
   v
  R1
   |
   v
  R2
   |
   v
  R3
or:
Primary
   |
   +--> regional replica
             |
             +--> local replicas
This can reduce the replication fan-out from one node.
For example:
US Primary
    |
    v
EU Regional Replica
   / \
  v   v
EU1 EU2
This is particularly interesting for geographically distributed systems.
________________________________________
31. Why hierarchical replication?
Imagine:
1 primary
100 replicas
If the primary directly streams to all 100:
Primary
 | | | | | | | ...
the replication fan-out can become expensive.
Instead:
Primary
   |
   +--> Regional replicas
             |
             +--> local replicas
Now regional nodes act as replication intermediaries.
This can help with:
•	network topology 
•	bandwidth 
•	regional scaling 
•	fault isolation 
but increases architectural complexity.
________________________________________
32. Production architecture #9: Read replicas behind a proxy
From the Java application's perspective, you may not want:
connectionToPrimary();
connectionToReplica1();
connectionToReplica2();
Instead:
Java application
       |
       v
Database proxy/router
       |
    +--+--+
    |     |
    v     v
Primary Replicas
The proxy may perform:
write → primary
read  → replicas
and potentially:
health checks
failover
topology discovery
connection routing
This makes application code simpler.
But the proxy becomes another important infrastructure component.
________________________________________
33. Java-specific production issue: connection pools
This is important for your background.
Suppose:
Spring Boot
HikariCP
has a pool connected to:
Primary P
Primary dies.
A failover mechanism promotes:
R1 → new primary
But your existing connections may still point to:
P
Therefore:
database failover
doesn't automatically mean:
application failover
You need:
topology-aware driver
proxy
DNS/service discovery
connection retry
pool refresh
depending on the technology.
________________________________________
34. The application must understand topology changes
A production Java system should be designed around:
Primary is not permanent.
That is a powerful mental model.
Instead:
current primary = P
At time T1.
Later:
current primary = R1
At time T2.
Therefore:
connection string
should often represent:
how to discover the current database topology
rather than:
"this exact machine is the database."
________________________________________
35. Failure detection
How does the system know:
Primary is dead
?
Possibilities include:
heartbeat
health checks
timeouts
failure detectors
consensus
external orchestrator
But here's the distributed-systems problem:
A timeout doesn't prove that a node is dead.
It may simply be:
slow
network partitioned
GC pause
CPU overloaded
packet loss
This is why failure detection is fundamentally tricky.
________________________________________
36. The dangerous scenario
Suppose:
Primary P
Replica R
Network between them fails.
P thinks:
R is dead
R thinks:
P is dead
Application traffic somehow reaches both.
If both become writable:
P → W1
R → W2
Now you have split brain.
Production architectures spend significant effort preventing this.
________________________________________
37. Quorum helps prevent split brain
Suppose:
A
B
C
Leadership requires:
2/3
If partition is:
A | B C
then:
B+C = majority
A   = minority
Only B+C can elect a leader.
A cannot obtain the required quorum.
Thus:
A → reject writes
B+C → continue
This is one of the fundamental reasons quorum-based consensus is powerful.
________________________________________
38. Odd numbers of nodes
You will often see:
3
5
7
node consensus clusters.
Why?
Because majority is:
3 → 2
5 → 3
7 → 4
For 3 nodes:
2 required
You tolerate:
1 failure
For 5:
3 required
You tolerate:
2 failures
But don't blindly add nodes.
More nodes can mean:
more network traffic
more coordination
more operational complexity
You choose based on failure requirements.
________________________________________
39. Failure tolerance formula
For a simple majority quorum:
N = 2F + 1
where:
F = failures tolerated
Examples:
F=1 → N=3
F=2 → N=5
F=3 → N=7
Because you need:
F+1
nodes to maintain majority.
This is one of those formulas worth remembering.
________________________________________
40. But geographic placement matters more than node count
Suppose:
3 nodes
all in:
one AZ
You can tolerate:
one machine failure
but maybe not:
AZ failure
Better:
AZ-A → Node A
AZ-B → Node B
AZ-C → Node C
Now a single AZ outage may still leave:
2/3
and therefore a majority.
This is failure-domain-aware replication.
________________________________________
41. Region-level consensus is much harder
Suppose:
US
EU
Asia
with one consensus node each.
If the US ↔ EU network is interrupted:
US | EU + Asia
the EU+Asia side may retain majority.
But if your network partition splits:
US + EU | Asia
the two-node side can continue while Asia cannot.
This affects:
availability
latency
quorum
disaster recovery
and is why global consensus systems require careful topology design.
________________________________________
42. Production architecture #10: Sharding + replication
Now we're approaching the architecture you'll encounter in serious distributed databases.
Replication handles:
same data
multiple copies
Sharding handles:
different data
different nodes
Combine them:
                   Cluster
             +-------+-------+
             |               |
          Shard 1          Shard 2
          / | \             / | \
         P  R1 R2          P  R1 R2
Now:
Shard 1:
customers 1-1M

Shard 2:
customers 1M-2M
and each shard is itself replicated.
This is a huge architectural jump.
________________________________________
43. Why replication + sharding is powerful
Without sharding:
all data
   ↓
one primary
Eventually:
storage bottleneck
CPU bottleneck
IOPS bottleneck
With sharding:
Shard A → Primary + replicas
Shard B → Primary + replicas
Shard C → Primary + replicas
Now you can scale:
write throughput
storage
CPU
horizontally.
But operational complexity increases dramatically.
________________________________________
44. A realistic production cluster
Imagine:
                  Application
                       |
                  DB Router
                       |
       +---------------+---------------+
       |               |               |
     Shard 1         Shard 2         Shard 3
       |               |               |
      P/R/R           P/R/R           P/R/R
       |               |               |
      AZs             AZs             AZs
Now each shard has:
leader
replicas
failover
consistency
and the whole cluster additionally has:
routing
partitioning
rebalancing
cross-shard operations
This is what makes distributed databases substantially harder than ordinary replication.
________________________________________
45. Cross-shard transactions
Suppose:
User = Shard 1
Order = Shard 2
Payment = Shard 3
Now:
create order
+
debit payment
+
update user
may involve three independent replication groups.
A local transaction is no longer enough.
You may need:
distributed transaction
2PC
sagas
outbox
event-driven coordination
This is where replication architecture starts interacting with transaction architecture.
________________________________________
46. A key production pattern: quorum locally, async globally
A sophisticated architecture might look like:
                     Region A
                +----------------+
                |                |
                | Leader         |
                |  / \           |
                | R1  R2         |
                +----------------+
                     |
                  async
                     |
                     v
                     Region B
                +----------------+
                | Leader/Replica |
                |  / \           |
                | R1  R2         |
                +----------------+
Within a region:
stronger synchronous/quorum replication
Across regions:
asynchronous replication
This gives a useful balance:
local durability
+
local HA
+
cross-region disaster recovery
without putting intercontinental latency into every write.
________________________________________
47. What happens when Region A disappears?
Suppose:
Region A X
Region B has:
last replicated position = 900
while Region A had:
position = 920
Potentially:
900–920
is lost.
So the failover process must decide:
promote B
and accept:
RPO = 20 log entries
or perhaps:
wait for another surviving copy
if one exists.
This is a business decision as much as a technical one.
________________________________________
48. Disaster recovery architecture
For many organizations:
Production Region
       |
       | async replication
       v
DR Region
The DR region is not serving normal traffic.
Its purpose:
regional disaster
      ↓
promote DR
      ↓
restore service
You should distinguish:
HA
Protects against:
machine/AZ failure
DR
Protects against:
regional catastrophe
They are not the same thing.
________________________________________
49. HA vs DR
A useful mental table:
	HA	DR
Typical failure	node/AZ	region
Goal	quick continuity	disaster survival
Typical replication	sync/local	often async
RTO	seconds/minutes	minutes/hours
RPO	near-zero possible	often non-zero
Cost	higher	substantial
Actual numbers depend on the architecture and business requirements.
________________________________________
50. Backup is still necessary
This is another important production lesson.
Replication is not backup.
Suppose an application accidentally executes:
DELETE FROM customers;
Replication faithfully propagates:
DELETE
   ↓
R1
R2
R3
Now every replica is wrong.
Replication protects against:
node failure
It does not necessarily protect against:
logical corruption
operator error
bad deployment
malicious deletion
application bug
That's why production systems need:
replication
+
backups
+
point-in-time recovery
________________________________________
51. The three layers of protection
A mature production architecture often has:
                DATA PROTECTION
                      |
          +-----------+-----------+
          |           |           |
         HA           DR        Backup
          |           |           |
       node/AZ      region      logical error
       failure      failure      recovery
This is a much better mental model than:
"We have replicas, so we're safe."
________________________________________
52. Production read routing
Let's design a real read router.
Suppose:
Primary P
R1 lag = 10 ms
R2 lag = 50 ms
R3 lag = 3 sec
Request:
GET product
max staleness = 5 sec
All replicas qualify.
Router might choose:
R1
Now:
GET account balance
consistency = strong
Router:
P
Now:
GET order immediately after write
requiredVersion = 500
Router checks:
R1 = 501 → eligible
R2 = 480 → not eligible
R3 = 300 → not eligible
and routes:
→ R1
This is the kind of architecture you should be able to design at senior/staff level.
________________________________________
53. Production topology discovery
How does the router know:
P
R1
R2
R3
?
Possibilities:
database-native topology discovery
service discovery
DNS
proxy
orchestrator
control plane
When failover occurs:
P X
R1 → Primary
the topology changes.
The router must eventually learn:
old P = dead
R1 = primary
This is a control-plane problem.
________________________________________
54. Data plane vs control plane
A useful distinction:
Data plane
Actual traffic:
WRITE
READ
Control plane
Manages:
leader election
topology
failover
health
membership
configuration
Architecture:
             Control Plane
                  |
          topology / leadership
                  |
                  v
             Data Plane
          /      |      \
        P        R1      R2
A database can have excellent data replication but still have poor failover if the control plane is unreliable.
________________________________________
55. The failure sequence you should visualize
Let's walk through a production failure.
Normal:
             P
           /   \
         R1     R2
Application:
write → P
read  → R1/R2
Now P fails:
             X
           /   \
          R1    R2
Failure detector says:
P unavailable
Election:
R1 wins
Then:
             R1
              |
              v
             R2
Router updates:
writes → R1
reads  → R1/R2
Applications refresh connections.
Old P must not regain write authority.
When P returns:
old P
   ↓
rejoin as replica
not:
old P → become primary automatically
This sequence is critical.
________________________________________
56. Why old primary should usually rejoin as replica
Suppose old P's state is:
version 100
New primary R1:
version 120
If old P simply becomes primary again:
P = 100
R1 = 120
you could lose data.
Instead:
P rejoins
  ↓
discard/reconcile stale state
  ↓
catch up from R1
  ↓
becomes replica
This is a very common failover pattern.
________________________________________
57. Split brain prevention is non-negotiable
A production design review should always ask:
What prevents two primaries?
Possible answers:
quorum
consensus
fencing
epoch numbers
leases
external coordination
If the answer is:
"We check if the old primary is alive."
That's not sufficient.
Because distributed systems cannot reliably distinguish:
dead
from:
temporarily unreachable
using a simple network timeout.
