Lesson 1 — Leader/Follower Architecture
1. Start with the problem
Imagine a distributed database with one machine:
             Client
                |
                v
        +---------------+
        |    Database   |
        |     Node A    |
        +---------------+
Initially this is simple.
A client does:
WRITE user 123
Node A changes its state:
user[123] = "Alice"
Now imagine Node A dies.
             Client
                X
                |
                v
        +---------------+
        |    Node A     |  💀
        +---------------+
Your database is unavailable.
So we introduce replication.
                 +-------------+
                 |   Node A    |
                 +-------------+
                  /           \
                 /             \
                v               v
        +-------------+   +-------------+
        |   Node B    |   |   Node C    |
        +-------------+   +-------------+
Now the data exists in multiple places.
But we have a new problem:
Who is allowed to decide the authoritative order of writes?
Suppose two clients simultaneously do:
Client 1: balance = 100
Client 2: balance = 200
If every replica independently accepts writes:
Node A -> 100
Node B -> 200
Node C -> 100
Now the replicas disagree.
Replication solved durability/availability, but introduced a coordination problem.
This is where leader/follower architecture enters.
________________________________________
2. Core idea
In a leader/follower architecture, replicas have asymmetric responsibilities.
There is normally:
                 +----------------+
                 |    LEADER      |
                 |                |
                 | accepts writes |
                 | orders writes  |
                 +-------+--------+
                         |
                 replication
                    /          \
                   v            v
          +-------------+  +-------------+
          |  FOLLOWER   |  |  FOLLOWER   |
          |             |  |             |
          | applies log |  | applies log |
          +-------------+  +-------------+
The important concept isn't merely:
Leader writes, followers read.
The deeper concept is:
The leader establishes a single authoritative order of state transitions, and followers reproduce that order.
That sentence is worth remembering.
________________________________________
3. What exactly is being replicated?
This is where many explanations become too shallow.
People often say:
"The leader sends data to followers."
Not necessarily.
A better model is:
Client command
      |
      v
   Leader
      |
      v
 ordered operations
      |
      v
 Replication log
      |
      +------> Follower 1
      |
      +------> Follower 2
For example:
SET balance = 100
SET balance = 150
SET balance = 80
The leader establishes:
Operation #101 -> balance = 100
Operation #102 -> balance = 150
Operation #103 -> balance = 80
Followers apply operations in that order.
Therefore:
Leader:
101
102
103

Follower 1:
101
102
103

Follower 2:
101
102
103
The database state emerges from applying the same ordered operations.
________________________________________
4. Leader is fundamentally an ordering authority
This is probably the most important conceptual upgrade.
Suppose we have:
A = balance $100
Two requests arrive:
R1: withdraw $30
R2: withdraw $50
The system needs an ordering.
Possibility 1:
R1
R2

100 - 30 - 50 = 20
Possibility 2:
R2
R1

100 - 50 - 30 = 20
Here the result happens to be identical.
But consider:
R1: SET balance = 30
R2: SET balance = 50
Order matters:
R1 -> R2
final = 50
versus:
R2 -> R1
final = 30
The leader gives the distributed system a notion of:
"This happened before that."
That's why leader-based replication is powerful.
________________________________________
5. Leader/follower isn't the same thing as backup
This distinction is extremely important.
A naive mental model is:
Leader = primary database
Follower = backup database
That's incomplete.
A follower is typically a live replica, not merely a passive backup.
For example:
Leader:
LSN 100

Follower A:
LSN 98

Follower B:
LSN 100
Follower B is essentially caught up.
Follower A is two operations behind.
This gives us the concept of replication lag.
Leader LSN = 100

Follower A LSN = 100   lag = 0
Follower B LSN = 97    lag = 3
This lag becomes extremely important in Lesson 3 when we discuss reads.
________________________________________
6. The leader and follower have state
Think of each node as having a state machine.
For example:
Initial:

balance = 100
The leader receives:
withdraw 30
It produces:
log entry #501:
    withdraw 30
and applies it:
balance = 70
Follower receives the same operation:
log entry #501:
    withdraw 30
and applies:
balance = 70
So:
                operation #501
                      |
          +-----------+-----------+
          |                       |
          v                       v
       Leader                 Follower
       apply                  apply
          |                       |
          v                       v
      balance=70             balance=70
This is essentially a replicated state machine idea.
The abstraction is:
Same initial state + same ordered operations → same resulting state.
That idea appears far beyond databases.
It is foundational in distributed systems.
________________________________________
7. Three separate concepts: replication, leadership, consensus
These are often mixed together.
They are different.
Replication
Means:
Keep copies of state on multiple nodes.
A ---> B
 \---> C
Leadership
Means:
One node is currently responsible for coordinating/ordering operations.
       Leader
       /    \
      v      v
   Follower Follower
Consensus
Means:
Multiple nodes agree on a value/order despite failures.
Examples include algorithms such as Raft and Paxos.
So:
Replication != Leadership != Consensus
However, production systems frequently combine them.
For example, a Raft-based database might use:
Consensus
    +
Leader election
    +
Replicated log
to create a fault-tolerant leader/follower system.
________________________________________
8. Why not allow every replica to accept writes?
Let's explore the alternative.
Suppose:
       Client A
          |
          v
     +---------+
     | Node A  |
     +---------+

       Client B
          |
          v
     +---------+
     | Node B  |
     +---------+
Both accept writes.
Initial:
x = 0
Requests:
A: x = 10
B: x = 20
Now:
Node A -> x = 10
Node B -> x = 20
How do they resolve this?
They need some mechanism to determine:
10 happened before 20
or:
20 happened before 10
Now you have entered the world of multi-leader replication, conflict resolution, distributed transactions, consensus, logical clocks, CRDTs, etc.
Those systems can absolutely work.
But they're considerably more complicated.
Leader/follower says:
Let's reduce the write-ordering problem by having one authority for writes.
________________________________________
9. The basic architecture
A production-oriented abstraction looks more like this:
                         Clients
                            |
                            v
                    +---------------+
                    | Load Balancer  |
                    +-------+-------+
                            |
                            v
                    +---------------+
                    |    LEADER     |
                    |               |
                    | write owner   |
                    +-------+-------+
                            |
                 replication log
                  /        |        \
                 v         v         v
          +----------+ +----------+ +----------+
          |Follower 1| |Follower 2| |Follower 3|
          +----------+ +----------+ +----------+
                |
                |
           read traffic
But don't memorize that picture as universal architecture.
There are many variations.
For example:
Writes
   |
   v
Leader

Reads
 |     |      |
 v     v      v
F1    F2     F3
or:
Writes ---> Leader
Reads  ---> Leader
or:
Writes ---> Leader
Reads  ---> Leader + selected followers
The choice depends on the consistency guarantees you want.
________________________________________
10. The most important invariant
A useful invariant for a leader/follower replicated log is:
Followers must not invent an independent history that conflicts with the authoritative history.
Imagine:
Leader log:

100: INSERT A
101: UPDATE A
102: DELETE A
Follower:
100: INSERT A
101: UPDATE A
102: DELETE A
Good.
But imagine:
Leader:

100: INSERT A
101: UPDATE A
102: DELETE A
Follower:
100: INSERT A
101: UPDATE A
That's okay temporarily.
It's behind.
But:
Follower:

100: INSERT A
101: UPDATE A
102: UPDATE A differently
That's dangerous.
It has diverged.
A major job of the replication protocol is therefore to maintain log consistency and recover from divergence.
________________________________________
11. Synchronous vs asynchronous followers
Now we reach one of the biggest architectural decisions.
Asynchronous replication
Leader commits locally and doesn't wait for followers.
Client
  |
  v
Leader
  |
  +----> commit
  |
  +----> Follower 1
  |
  +----> Follower 2
Example:
t0: client -> leader
t1: leader writes
t2: leader acknowledges client
t3: follower receives operation
Advantages:
•	lower write latency 
•	higher throughput 
•	leader doesn't depend on follower response 
But:
A successful client response does not necessarily mean followers have the data.
This creates a failure scenario:
Leader:
operation #500 committed

Follower:
only has #499
Leader suddenly dies.
The new leader might only know up to:
#499
Depending on the system's election/replication guarantees, operation #500 may be lost from the surviving replica set.
That's a huge consistency issue.
________________________________________
12. Synchronous replication
Now imagine the leader waits for a follower.
Client
  |
  v
Leader
  |
  | operation #500
  v
Follower
  |
  | ACK
  v
Leader
  |
  v
Client ACK
Now the client response can mean something stronger:
The operation has been replicated to at least one additional node.
This improves durability.
But there is a price:
write latency
    +
network latency
    +
follower availability
If the follower is slow:
Leader
   |
   |---- 2 ms
   |
Follower
   |
   |---- 100 ms
your write may now take ~100 ms rather than ~2 ms.
And if the follower is unavailable, what happens?
Do we:
reject writes?
or:
continue with reduced durability?
That is a system design decision, not an implementation detail.
________________________________________
13. Quorum-based thinking
Many distributed systems generalize this further.
Suppose we have:
N = 3 replicas

Leader
Follower A
Follower B
We may require:
W = 2
replicas to acknowledge a write.
For example:
Leader      ACK
Follower A  ACK
Follower B  no response
We have:
2/3
So the write succeeds.
This is quorum-style thinking.
A common rule is:
W + R > N
for certain quorum consistency models.
But be careful: this formula alone doesn't magically guarantee strong consistency. The semantics depend on exactly what "read" and "write" acknowledgements mean, how versions are ordered, failure handling, membership changes, and the replication protocol.
This is why distributed databases cannot be understood purely through formulas.
________________________________________
14. What does "leader" actually mean?
This depends on the database.
In one system, leader may mean:
Only this node accepts writes for a shard.
In another:
Leader proposes entries into a replicated consensus log.
In another:
Primary handles writes and asynchronously ships a WAL to replicas.
So "leader" is an architectural role, not one universal implementation.
Think of the abstraction as:
                LEADER
                   |
        +----------+----------+
        |          |          |
      order      accept     replicate
      writes     writes      state
The exact mechanisms vary.
________________________________________
15. Leader/follower at shard level
Here's where real distributed databases get interesting.
Suppose we have 1 billion users.
One leader cannot necessarily handle everything.
We partition the data.
                Database
                   |
       +-----------+-----------+
       |           |           |
     Shard 1     Shard 2     Shard 3
       |           |           |
       v           v           v
    Leader       Leader       Leader
      / \          / \          / \
     F1  F2       F1  F2       F1  F2
Now there isn't necessarily one global leader.
There may be:
Shard 1 -> Leader A
Shard 2 -> Leader B
Shard 3 -> Leader C
This distinction is extremely important.
When we later discuss distributed databases, ask:
Leader for what?
It may be:
•	leader for the entire database 
•	leader for a partition 
•	leader for a replication group 
•	leader for a consensus group 
Modern distributed databases often use leader per shard/partition.
________________________________________
16. Example: e-commerce system
Suppose we have:
Product inventory:

SKU = iPhone-XYZ
stock = 10
Two customers simultaneously purchase one.
Requests:
R1: stock--
R2: stock--
With a leader:
                  Leader
                    |
              ordered writes
                 /     \
                v       v
               F1       F2
Leader orders:
#1001 stock--
#1002 stock--
State becomes:
10
 ↓ #1001
9
 ↓ #1002
8
Followers reproduce:
10
 ↓
9
 ↓
8
The leader therefore provides a single serialization point for that shard.
________________________________________
17. But a leader is a bottleneck
This is the major downside.
Suppose:
             1 million writes/sec
                     |
                     v
                  Leader
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          v          v          v
         F1         F2         F3
All writes are going through one node.
Eventually:
CPU       -> bottleneck
Network   -> bottleneck
Disk/WAL  -> bottleneck
Locking   -> bottleneck
That's why systems scale horizontally using partitioning:
                    Writes
                       |
       +---------------+---------------+
       |               |               |
       v               v               v
    Leader A        Leader B        Leader C
    Shard 1         Shard 2         Shard 3
Now write load is distributed.
________________________________________
