19. The deeper mental model
Don't think:
Leader = master
Follower = slave
Instead think:
               Replicated State Machine

                       Leader
                         |
                 establishes order
                         |
                   replicated log
                    /          \
                   v            v
              Follower       Follower
                   |              |
             apply entries   apply entries
                   |              |
                   v              v
                State          State
The fundamental pipeline is:
COMMAND
   ↓
ORDER
   ↓
LOG
   ↓
REPLICATION
   ↓
APPLY
   ↓
STATE
That pipeline will become the backbone for all five lessons.
________________________________________
20. One subtle but critical distinction: log vs database state
Suppose:
Database state:

balance = 80
You might think that's what gets replicated.
But often the more useful conceptual model is:
Log:

#1 SET balance=100
#2 WITHDRAW 20
and then:
State = apply(log)
Why is the log useful?
Because if a follower is behind:
Leader:
#1 #2 #3 #4 #5

Follower:
#1 #2 #3
the leader can conceptually send:
#4
#5
rather than sending the entire database again.
This also enables:
•	recovery 
•	replay 
•	crash recovery 
•	replication 
•	auditing 
•	catch-up 
•	leader changes 
The exact mechanism differs across databases, but the abstraction is extraordinarily useful.
________________________________________
21. CAP connection
Leader/follower architectures also force you to think about network partitions.
Imagine:
              Network partition
                   X X X

        +---------+     +---------+
        | Leader  |     |Follower |
        +---------+     +---------+
The leader can't communicate with the follower.
Now should the leader continue accepting writes?
If yes:
Leader continues
you may preserve availability but risk problems with replication/durability depending on the system.
If no:
Leader stops writes
you sacrifice availability to preserve stronger consistency guarantees.
This is one reason distributed database design is fundamentally about failure behavior, not just normal operation.
________________________________________
22. Java developer translation
Since you're coming from Java, think about leader/follower as an abstraction above something like:
interface ReplicationNode {
    void append(LogEntry entry);
    void apply(LogEntry entry);
}
Leader responsibilities might conceptually be:
class Leader {

    LogPosition append(Command command) {
        LogEntry entry = log.append(command);
        replicate(entry);
        return entry.position();
    }
}
Follower:
class Follower {

    void receive(LogEntry entry) {
        log.append(entry);
        apply(entry);
    }
}
But do not implement a real distributed database this way without solving:
•	duplicate messages 
•	message reordering 
•	retries 
•	crashes 
•	partial writes 
•	persistent log 
•	acknowledgements 
•	leader epochs/terms 
•	stale leaders 
•	membership changes 
•	network partitions 
•	election 
•	quorum 
•	log divergence 
That's precisely where distributed systems become difficult.
________________________________________
23. The three questions you should always ask
When you see a leader/follower database design, don't ask only:
"Where are the reads and writes?"
Ask these three:
Question 1 — Who establishes ordering?
Who decides:

A happened before B?
Question 2 — When is an operation considered committed?
Leader wrote it?

Leader + 1 follower?

Majority?

All replicas?
Question 3 — What happens when the leader disappears?
Who can become leader?

What data can that leader safely expose?

What prevents two leaders?
Those three questions take you much deeper than the usual interview-level explanation.
