18. Now let's break it
This is where distributed systems becomes interesting.
The happy path is easy.
Failures are the real lesson.
________________________________________
Failure 1 — Leader crashes before writing the log
Timeline:
t1 Client -> Leader
t2 Leader validates
t3 Leader crashes
No log entry exists.
Result:
write not committed
Client may receive:
timeout
The client now has an ambiguity:
Did the database process my request or not?
This is a major distributed-systems problem.
________________________________________
Failure 2 — Leader writes WAL but crashes before replication
Timeline:
t1 Leader receives request
t2 Leader writes #1004 to local WAL
t3 Leader crashes
t4 Followers only have #1003
Now:
Leader:
#1004

Followers:
#1003
Was #1004 committed?
If not, a new leader can safely exclude it.
This is why:
Local durability and distributed durability are different things.
________________________________________
Failure 3 — Leader replicates to one follower but crashes
Leader A:
#1004

Follower B:
#1004

Follower C:
#1003

A 💀
Now B has the newest entry.
If the system requires majority and B + A formed the majority before A died, the protocol may preserve #1004.
This is precisely why replication protocols carefully define:
•	quorum 
•	commitment 
•	election eligibility 
•	log matching 
________________________________________
Failure 4 — Leader sends ACK to client too early
Imagine:
Leader
  |
  | writes locally
  |
  +----> Client SUCCESS
  |
  X
  💀
Followers never received the write.
Now client believes:
WRITE SUCCESS
but the surviving cluster doesn't contain it.
That's a dangerous design if you promise strong durability.
So the write response semantics are tightly connected to replication.
________________________________________
19. Client retry creates another problem
This one is extremely important for real systems.
Suppose:
Client -> Leader:
WITHDRAW $100
Leader commits it.
But before response reaches client:
Leader ----X----> Client
Client sees:
TIMEOUT
What does the client do?
Usually:
retry
Now the database receives:
WITHDRAW $100
WITHDRAW $100
Oops.
Account:
1000
 ↓ first request
900
 ↓ retry
800
But client intended:
1000 -> 900
This is why idempotency matters.
________________________________________
20. Request IDs
A common solution is to give the operation a unique ID:
request_id = abc123
Client sends:
WITHDRAW
account=42
amount=100
request_id=abc123
Leader records:
abc123 -> committed
If the client retries:
request_id=abc123
the database can recognize:
"I've already processed this operation."
Then:
first request:
execute

retry:
return previous result
This pattern is incredibly useful in distributed systems.
________________________________________
21. Timeout does not mean failure
Memorize this.
A timeout is not the same as a failed operation.
Consider:
Client
  |
  | WRITE
  v
Leader
  |
  | commit
  v
Database
  |
  X
  |
Client never receives response
Client sees:
TIMEOUT
But database state is:
WRITE = SUCCESS
Therefore the client cannot infer:
timeout => operation didn't happen
The correct state is:
timeout => outcome unknown
This is one of the most important practical lessons in distributed systems.
________________________________________
22. The write path and linearizability
Now let's connect this to a formal consistency property.
Suppose a client performs:
WRITE x = 10
and receives:
SUCCESS
Then immediately:
READ x
If the system promises linearizability, the read should not return an older value such as:
x = 5
even if it goes to a follower.
Why?
Because the successful write logically took effect at some point between invocation and response.
This becomes extremely important in Lesson 3.
________________________________________
23. Write concern
Different systems expose some notion of write concern.
Conceptually:
write concern = how much replication/durability do I require?
Examples might be:
LOCAL
meaning:
leader has persisted it locally
or:
MAJORITY
meaning:
required majority has acknowledged it
or some system-specific policy such as:
2 replicas
This creates a direct trade-off:
stronger durability
       ↑
       |
       |       latency
       |
       ↓
weaker durability
You don't get stronger guarantees for free.
________________________________________
24. Synchronous vs asynchronous write path
Let's compare them.
Asynchronous
Client
  |
  v
Leader
  |
  | persist
  |
  +----> Client ACK
  |
  +----> Follower
Latency:
low
But replication can lag.
________________________________________
Synchronous / quorum-based
Client
  |
  v
Leader
  |
  +----> Follower B
  |          |
  |         ACK
  |          |
  +----------+
  |
  v
Client ACK
Latency:
higher
But stronger durability/commit semantics can be provided.
________________________________________
25. Why "wait for all followers" is usually bad
Suppose:
Leader
 |
 +--> F1  5 ms
 |
 +--> F2  6 ms
 |
 +--> F3  8 ms
 |
 +--> F4 500 ms
If you require every follower:
write latency ≈ 500 ms
One slow replica becomes a write bottleneck.
If instead you require a quorum:
Leader + enough followers
you can tolerate a slow/dead replica.
This is one reason quorum-based replication is so powerful.
________________________________________
26. Quorum mathematics
Suppose:
N = 5
replicas.
Majority:
floor(N/2) + 1
= 3
So:
3 replicas
are sufficient for a majority.
Why is majority interesting?
Because any two majorities overlap.
For N = 5:
Majority A = {A,B,C}
Majority B = {C,D,E}
They share:
C
That overlap is extremely important for maintaining a single authoritative history.
More generally, two majorities of the same replica set cannot be disjoint.
This mathematical property becomes fundamental in leader election and consensus.
________________________________________
27. A subtle point: replication ≠ consensus
Suppose leader asynchronously replicates:
A -> B
A -> C
That's replication.
But suppose A dies and B says:
"I'm the leader now."
How do we know B is allowed to become leader?
What if C says:
"No, I'm the leader."
Now we need an agreement mechanism.
That is where consensus enters.
So:
Write path:
       |
       +-- replication
       |
       +-- commitment
       |
       +-- consensus rules
depending on the database architecture.
________________________________________
28. Leader terms/epochs
One concept you'll need for Lesson 4 is the idea of a leader generation.
Imagine:
Leader A
term = 7
Then A dies.
New election:
Leader B
term = 8
Now suppose an old delayed message from A arrives:
A(term=7) -> "WRITE X"
B can reason:
term 7 < term 8
Therefore:
reject stale leader operation
This is a beautiful technique because it turns:
"Is this node still leader?"
into something the protocol can reason about.
We'll go much deeper into this in Lesson 4.
________________________________________
29. The complete write path with failure information
Here's the model I'd like you to keep in your head:
                   CLIENT
                      |
                      | request
                      v
              +---------------+
              |    LEADER     |
              +-------+-------+
                      |
                validation
                      |
                      v
                assign order
                      |
                      v
                append WAL
                      |
                      v
               replication
                 /       \
                v         v
              F1          F2
                \         /
                 \       /
                  ACKs
                    |
                    v
                COMMIT
                    |
                    v
                 APPLY
                    |
                    v
               CLIENT ACK
Every stage can fail:
client -> leader
leader -> WAL
leader -> follower
follower -> leader
leader -> client
And every failure can produce ambiguity.
________________________________________
30. The three "truths" you should distinguish
For a write W, ask:
Truth #1 — Has the leader seen it?
received = yes
Truth #2 — Has it been durably recorded?
WAL = yes
Truth #3 — Is it committed?
commit = yes
These can be different.
For example:
received = yes
WAL = yes
committed = no
or:
received = yes
WAL = yes
replicated = yes
committed = yes
A strong distributed-systems engineer always asks:
"What exactly does success mean?"
________________________________________
31. A Java analogy
Think of a local transaction:
transaction.begin();

updateAccount();

transaction.commit();
In a single-node database, commit() already hides a lot.
In a distributed replicated database, conceptually you might have:
appendToWAL(command);

replicate(command);

waitForRequiredAcks();

markCommitted();

apply(command);

returnSuccess();
The distributed version introduces:
network
+
other machines
+
failure
+
partial progress
+
leader changes
That's why distributed commit is fundamentally harder than local commit.
________________________________________
32. Production debugging mindset
When debugging a write problem in a distributed database, don't just ask:
"Did the write fail?"
Ask:
1. Did request reach the leader?
2. Which leader/term handled it?
3. Was it assigned a log position?
4. Was it persisted?
5. Which followers received it?
6. Which followers acknowledged it?
7. Was it committed?
8. Was it applied?
9. Did the client receive the response?
10. Did the client retry?
11. Did leadership change afterward?
This is the kind of reasoning that separates:
"I know distributed database terminology"
from:
"I can diagnose distributed database behavior."
________________________________________
33. The most important failure scenario
Let's finish with one scenario that ties everything together.
Three nodes:
A = Leader
B = Follower
C = Follower
Client sends:
WRITE X = 10
Leader creates:
#501 X=10
Then:
A -> B   #501
A -> C   #501
B acknowledges.
Suppose C is slow.
Now:
A = #501
B = #501
C = #500
If the system's commitment rule is majority:
A + B = 2/3
so #501 can become committed.
Then:
A -> Client: SUCCESS
Now A crashes.
A 💀
B still has:
#501
Therefore B has evidence of the committed history.
This is the bridge to the next lesson:
How do we safely decide that B—not C—should become the next leader?
And even more importantly:
How do we prevent C from becoming leader while B is also acting as leader?
That's leader election + split brain, which is the heart of Lessons 4 and 5.
________________________________________
What you should be able to explain after Lesson 2
If I asked you in an architecture interview:
"A client writes to a leader/follower database. What happens?"
A strong answer would be:
The client routes the write to the current leader. The leader validates the operation and establishes its position in the ordered replication log. The operation is persisted according to the database's durability rules and replicated to followers. Once the required replication/commit condition is satisfied—such as a majority—the operation becomes committed and can be applied/exposed according to the system's consistency semantics. The leader then acknowledges the client. Importantly, local WAL persistence, follower replication, commitment, application, and client acknowledgement are distinct events, and failures between them can make the client outcome ambiguous.
That's already much deeper than:
"The leader writes and replicates to followers."

Lesson 3 — Read Path
Lesson 2 gave us the write path:
Client
   ↓
Leader
   ↓
Log
   ↓
Followers
   ↓
Commit
Now comes the deceptively difficult question:
When a client wants to read data, which replica should answer, and what value is it allowed to return?
This is where leader/follower architecture turns into a consistency problem.
A beginner thinks:
Write → Leader
Read  → Follower
A distributed-systems engineer asks:
"How stale can that follower be, and what consistency guarantee does this read require?"
________________________________________
1. Start with our cluster
We'll use the same 3-node cluster:
                 +-------------+
                 |   Leader A  |
                 |   LSN = 500 |
                 +------+------+ 
                        |
                replication
                  /           \
                 v             v
        +-------------+ +-------------+
        | Follower B  | | Follower C  |
        | LSN = 500   | | LSN = 497   |
        +-------------+ +-------------+
Notice:
A = 500
B = 500
C = 497
C is three operations behind.
Now the client asks:
SELECT balance
FROM account
WHERE id = 42;
Which node should answer?
________________________________________
2. The simplest read path: always read from leader
The strongest/simple model is:
Client
   |
   | READ
   v
Leader
   |
   v
Database state
The leader has the authoritative current state.
Advantages:
•	simplest consistency model 
•	read sees committed leader state 
•	easy read-after-write behavior 
•	no follower-lag surprises 
But there's an obvious problem.
Suppose:
100,000 writes/sec
500,000 reads/sec
If everything goes through one leader:
                    Leader
                 /    |     \
              writes reads reads
The leader becomes a bottleneck.
So we want:
                 Leader
                    |
             replication
              /          \
             v            v
        Follower B   Follower C
             ↑            ↑
             |            |
           reads        reads
Now we gain read scalability.
But we introduce a new problem:
Followers may be behind.
________________________________________
3. Replication lag creates stale reads
Suppose the database starts with:
balance = 1000
Client writes:
WITHDRAW 100
Leader processes it:
Leader:
balance = 900
But follower C hasn't caught up:
Follower C:
balance = 1000
Now:
Client
   |
   | READ
   v
Follower C
   |
   v
1000
The client gets:
1000
even though the leader has:
900
That's a stale read.
Nothing necessarily "broke."
The follower is simply behind.
________________________________________
4. The first major distinction: correctness vs freshness
This is subtle.
A follower returning:
balance = 1000
when the latest leader state is:
balance = 900
is not necessarily corruption.
The follower's value may be perfectly correct according to its replicated history.
Its problem is:
It doesn't yet know about the newer operation.
So distinguish:
CORRUPT
from:
STALE
A stale replica can contain a completely valid historical state.
________________________________________
5. Read path with asynchronous replication
Suppose:
Leader:
LSN 500

Follower B:
LSN 498

Follower C:
LSN 497
Read routing:
             Client
                |
                v
          Read Router
           /        \
          v          v
         B            C
       498           497
The router may select C because:
•	C has lower CPU 
•	C is geographically closer 
•	load balancing selected it 
•	application explicitly chose it 
The read can therefore return data corresponding to LSN 497.
This gives us an important concept:
Read correctness depends on what consistency guarantee the application requires, not simply whether the node is a valid replica.
________________________________________
6. What does "latest" actually mean?
Suppose:
Leader:
x = 10

Follower:
x = 5
You say:
"The follower is stale."
But stale relative to what?
Distributed systems need a definition.
Possible meanings:
Latest according to local replica
Follower's latest state
Latest committed state
Latest state known to be committed
Latest globally observable state
Potentially stronger.
Latest after my previous write
Even stronger from the application's perspective.
These are different consistency requirements.
________________________________________
7. Read-after-write consistency
This is one of the most important concepts for application developers.
Suppose:
Client:
POST /profile
name = Alice
Write succeeds.
Then immediately:
GET /profile
The user expects:
{
  "name": "Alice"
}
But suppose the write went to leader:
Leader:
name = Alice
and the read goes to lagging follower:
Follower:
name = old value
The user sees:
name = Bob
This is extremely confusing.
From the application's perspective:
I just successfully changed my profile. Why did the next request show the old value?
That's the read-after-write problem.
________________________________________
8. Session consistency
A useful guarantee is:
Once a client has observed a write, subsequent reads from that client should not go backward behind that point.
For example:
Client
  |
  | WRITE X=10
  v
Leader
  |
  | success
  v
Client
  |
  | READ X
  v
Follower
We don't want:
READ -> X=5
after the client already observed:
WRITE -> X=10
This is often described as read-your-writes consistency.
It's weaker than full linearizability but very useful.
________________________________________
9. One simple solution: read from leader
If you require read-your-writes:
WRITE → Leader
READ  → Leader
then the problem becomes much easier.
Client
  |
  +---- WRITE ---> Leader
  |
  +---- READ ----> Leader
The leader has the authoritative current state.
But you've sacrificed some read scaling.
This leads to an important architectural decision:
Do all reads require the strongest consistency, or can some reads tolerate staleness?
________________________________________
10. Different data has different consistency requirements
Consider an e-commerce application.
Product description
"Blue Nike shoe"
Being 500 ms stale is probably fine.
Inventory
stock = 1
Stale reads can be dangerous.
Payment status
payment = SUCCESS
You probably don't want:
payment = PENDING
five seconds after successful payment.
Social media like count
likes = 10,001
A small amount of staleness is probably acceptable.
So a real system may deliberately use:
strong reads:
    account balance
    payment status
    inventory

eventually consistent reads:
    analytics
    counters
    feeds
This is consistency as an application-level requirement.
________________________________________
11. Read scaling
Why bother reading from followers?
Suppose:
Read traffic = 900k/sec
Leader capacity:
300k reads/sec
With three replicas:
Leader      300k
Follower 1  300k
Follower 2  300k
Potential aggregate capacity:
~900k reads/sec
The exact numbers are illustrative, but the architecture is the important part.
We have moved from:
                    Leader
                900k reads
to:
                 Read Router
               /      |      \
              v       v       v
             A        B       C
This is one of the major reasons leader/follower replication is popular.
________________________________________
12. But follower reads introduce a consistency budget
You can think of follower-based reads as spending a staleness budget.
For example:
Requirement:

data may be at most 1 second stale
Then a router could prefer followers whose replication lag is below:
1 second
Conceptually:
Follower A: 10 ms lag  ✓
Follower B: 200 ms lag ✓
Follower C: 4 sec lag  ✗
This is much more sophisticated than simply:
round-robin across replicas
________________________________________
13. Replication position as a read condition
Suppose a client performs a write at:
LSN = 500
Then it asks:
READ
We can conceptually attach:
required_position = 500
A follower can answer only if:
follower_position >= 500
So:
Follower B = 500
    ↓
can serve read

Follower C = 497
    ↓
must catch up
This is a powerful general technique.
Instead of saying:
"Please give me a fresh read."
we can say:
"Give me a read at least as fresh as position 500."
Different databases implement equivalent ideas differently.
________________________________________
14. This leads to a powerful mental model: read barriers
Suppose:
Client performed:
WRITE X=10

Write position = 500
Client then asks:
READ X
The system can enforce:
read position >= 500
If the selected follower is at:
497
it waits:
497
498
499
500
and then serves the read.
So:
Client
  |
  | required position=500
  v
Follower
  |
  | currently=497
  |
  | catch up
  v
500
  |
  v
READ
This gives read scaling without blindly accepting stale data.
________________________________________
15. Leader read vs follower read
Let's formalize the trade-off.
Read strategy	Freshness	Latency	Read scaling
Leader	Strong/current leader view	Low/medium	Limited
Follower	Potentially stale	Potentially low	High
Follower with freshness barrier	Controlled	May wait	High
Quorum-style	Stronger, depending on protocol	Higher	Moderate
Don't memorize the table as universal behavior.
The exact guarantee depends on the replication and consistency protocol.
________________________________________
16. Quorum reads
Suppose we have:
N = 3
replicas:
A
B
C
A quorum read might involve:
read from A
read from B
and compare versions/log positions.
Suppose:
A -> X=10, version 500
B -> X=10, version 500
Good.
But:
A -> X=10, version 500
B -> X=5,  version 497
The system can recognize:
500 > 497
and choose the newer version according to its consistency protocol.
But again:
Quorum read does not automatically mean linearizability.
You need to understand how versions, writes, membership, failures, and ordering interact.
________________________________________
17. Version numbers
One way to reason about replica state is:
value + version
Example:
Leader:
X = 10
version = 500

Follower:
X = 5
version = 497
The version tells us:
Follower is behind
This can be implemented conceptually with:
•	log positions 
•	commit indices 
•	timestamps 
•	transaction IDs 
•	logical clocks 
•	version vectors 
depending on the system.
________________________________________
18. Why timestamps are trickier than they look
A beginner may suggest:
timestamp = System.currentTimeMillis()
and say:
"We'll use the latest timestamp."
Distributed systems engineers immediately ask:
Are the clocks synchronized?
Suppose:
Machine A:
12:00:00.100

Machine B:
11:59:59.900
B's clock is 200 ms behind.
Now timestamp ordering becomes dangerous.
That's why distributed systems often use logical ordering mechanisms rather than relying purely on physical clocks.
We'll eventually get into:
•	Lamport clocks 
•	hybrid logical clocks 
•	causal ordering 
when we go deeper into consistency.
________________________________________
19. Linearizable reads
Now we reach a much stronger guarantee.
Linearizability says, roughly:
Each operation appears to take effect atomically at some point between its invocation and response, while respecting real-time ordering.
Suppose:
t1:
WRITE X=10
returns success.
Then:
t2:
READ X
The read should return:
10
not:
5
because the write completed before the read began.
This is stronger than simply saying:
"The replica will eventually receive the write."
________________________________________
20. Why leader reads are not automatically linearizable
This is subtle and important.
Suppose the client reads from the leader:
Client
  |
  v
Leader
You might think:
"Therefore it's linearizable."
Not necessarily.
Imagine:
Leader loses contact with quorum
but continues serving reads.
The leader's local state may be newer than followers, but the system may no longer have the authority to claim it is the current leader.
Another node might have been elected elsewhere.
So strong read semantics depend on:
•	current leadership 
•	quorum 
•	term/epoch 
•	commit state 
•	membership 
•	failure detection 
not simply:
if node == leader then strong read
________________________________________
21. The stale leader problem
This prepares us for Lesson 4.
Imagine:
A = leader
B = follower
C = follower
Network partition:
             X X X
        +---- network ----+
        |                 |
        A                 B,C
A cannot communicate with B/C.
Suppose B/C elect B as new leader.
Now:
A thinks:
"I'm leader."

B thinks:
"I'm leader."
If client reads from A:
READ -> A
and A has:
X = 100
while B has:
X = 120
Which answer is authoritative?
This is precisely why leader leases, terms, epochs, quorum reads, and consensus protocols matter.
We will dissect this in Lesson 4/5.
________________________________________
22. Read-your-writes through routing
A very common practical technique is:
After write:
    route user's reads to leader
For example:
User updates profile
       |
       v
Leader

For next few seconds:
       |
       v
Leader
After replication catches up:
reads can return to followers
Another technique is to carry a replication position:
client_context = LSN 500
and require:
read replica >= 500
This allows more flexible routing.
________________________________________
23. Causal consistency
Now consider two clients.
Client A:
WRITE post = "Hello"
Then:
READ comments
Client B sees the post and creates:
WRITE comment = "Nice!"
Now another client reads.
There is a causal relationship:
post
 ↓
comment
A system with causal consistency tries to preserve that relationship.
You don't want:
comment appears
before:
post appears
in contexts where the causal dependency should be preserved.
This is more advanced than simple leader/follower, but the read path is where these guarantees become visible to applications.
________________________________________
24. Read-your-writes vs linearizability vs eventual consistency
These are worth separating.
Eventual consistency
If writes stop:
eventually replicas converge
But a read immediately after a write might see old data.
________________________________________
Read-your-writes
A client won't see its own writes disappear behind an older version.
WRITE X=10
   ↓
READ X
   ↓
must see >= that version
________________________________________
Linearizability
All operations appear to happen in one globally consistent real-time order.
Much stronger.
A useful hierarchy intuition is:
Eventual
   ↓
Session guarantees / read-your-writes
   ↓
Causal consistency
   ↓
Linearizability
This isn't a universal strict hierarchy of every consistency model, but it's a useful learning progression.
________________________________________
25. Read routing is an architectural decision
Imagine:
                   Application
                       |
                 +-----+-----+
                 |           |
              writes       reads
                 |           |
                 v           v
              Leader     Read Router
                           /    \
                          v      v
                         F1      F2
The read router might consider:
replication lag
geographic distance
CPU
availability
consistency requirement
connection load
For example:
Strong read:
    Leader

Normal read:
    nearby follower

Analytics:
    replica with high lag acceptable
Therefore:
Read routing is part of the consistency architecture.
________________________________________
26. Geographic replication
Now make the system global.
                 US
                  |
               Leader
                  |
        +---------+---------+
        |                   |
        v                   v
      Europe              Asia
      Follower            Follower
Suppose:
US → Europe = 80 ms
US → Asia   = 150 ms
If every read goes to the leader:
Asia user → US leader → 150 ms+
But if Asia reads locally:
Asia user → Asia follower
latency could be dramatically lower.
However:
Asia follower may be stale
Now you have a classic trade-off:
lower latency
      ↕
stronger freshness
This is one reason globally distributed databases are fundamentally consistency/latency engineering problems.
________________________________________
27. A concrete example
Suppose:
Leader in Mumbai
Follower in Singapore
Follower in London
Client in Singapore:
WRITE order status = PAID
Write goes to Mumbai.
Then immediately:
GET order
goes to Singapore.
Timeline:
t0  write -> Mumbai
t1  Mumbai commits
t2  response -> client
t3  Singapore hasn't received update
t4  read -> Singapore
t5  returns PENDING
From a network perspective, nothing necessarily went wrong.
But from a user perspective:
"I paid, but the order says unpaid."
This is why consistency requirements must be designed explicitly.
________________________________________
28. The read path with a freshness requirement
Suppose the application says:
"I can tolerate up to 500 ms of staleness."
Then:
Follower A = 20 ms
Follower B = 150 ms
Follower C = 2 sec
Routing:
A ✓
B ✓
C ✗
This is a much more intelligent design than:
random follower
You can think of it as:
read eligibility =
    replica healthy
    AND lag <= allowed_staleness
________________________________________
29. What happens if no follower is fresh enough?
Suppose:
Leader = current
Follower A = 2 sec behind
Follower B = 5 sec behind
Requirement:
max_staleness = 500 ms
Now no follower qualifies.
The system must choose:
Option A:
read from leader

Option B:
wait for follower

Option C:
return error

Option D:
relax consistency requirement
There is no magical answer.
This is an architectural policy decision.
________________________________________
30. The "read from follower" misconception
A common interview answer:
"We use replicas to scale reads."
Follow-up question:
"What consistency do those reads provide?"
If the candidate can't answer, the design isn't complete.
You should always connect:
READ SCALING
      ↕
REPLICATION LAG
      ↕
CONSISTENCY
These three are inseparable.
________________________________________
31. A more realistic read architecture
A mature system may look like:
                           Application
                                |
                     +----------+----------+
                     |                     |
                  Strong                Relaxed
                   read                  read
                     |                     |
                     v                     v
                  Leader             Read Router
                                         |
                              +----------+----------+
                              |                     |
                              v                     v
                           Follower A           Follower B
                           lag=10ms              lag=50ms
And the application explicitly chooses:
readConsistency = STRONG
or:
readConsistency = EVENTUAL
or perhaps:
maxStaleness = 100ms
depending on the database/API.
________________________________________
32. The write/read interaction
This is perhaps the most important picture from Lessons 2 and 3.
Write:
Client
  |
  v
Leader
  |
  v
Log
  |
  +----> F1
  |
  +----> F2
  |
  v
Commit
Read:
Client
  |
  v
Read Router
 /    \
v      v
F1     F2
The problem is:
WRITE
  |
  v
Commit at position 500
  |
  X
  |
Follower only at 497
  |
  v
READ
Therefore:
WRITE succeeded
        +
READ from stale replica
        =
old value
That is the central problem of follower reads.
