40. What you should now be able to explain
If I ask:
"Why don't we simply send every read to followers?"
Your answer should be:
Because followers can lag behind the leader. Reading from a follower can therefore return stale state. Whether that's acceptable depends on the application's consistency requirement. For eventual or bounded-staleness reads, followers can provide excellent read scalability. For read-your-writes or linearizable reads, the system needs stronger routing or synchronization mechanisms, such as leader reads, replication-position barriers, quorum-based protocols, or other database-specific consistency mechanisms.
That's the core of Lesson 3.
