30. Raft election mechanics
Let's make it concrete.
Initial:
A = Leader, term 4
B = Follower, term 4
C = Follower, term 4
A crashes.
B's election timer expires.
B:
currentTerm = 5
state = Candidate
votedFor = B
B sends:
RequestVote(term=5, candidate=B, ...)
to C.
C checks:
5 > 4
and whether B's log is sufficiently up to date.
C grants vote.
Now:
B votes for B
C votes for B
Majority:
2/3
B becomes leader for term 5.
________________________________________
31. Why a node votes only once per term
Suppose C could vote for both B and D in term 5.
Then:
B gets C
D gets C
Potentially two candidates get majority.
So the protocol conceptually says:
one vote per node per term
That helps enforce:
At most one candidate can obtain a majority in a given term.
Combined with majority intersection, this is powerful.
________________________________________
32. Majority intersection
Let's prove the intuition.
N = 5.
Majority = 3.
Suppose B gets:
{A,B,C}
Could D also get:
{D,E,?}
It needs three votes.
The only remaining nodes after {A,B,C} are:
D,E
Only two.
Therefore D must share at least one voter with B.
But that voter cannot vote for both candidates in the same term.
Therefore both cannot legitimately obtain majority in the same term.
This is one of the fundamental mathematical foundations of quorum-based consensus.
________________________________________
33. But majority doesn't mean "more than half the machines are alive"
Be precise.
For N=5:
majority = 3
If only:
A,B
are alive:
2/5
No majority.
The system may be unable to elect a new leader.
This means:
A consensus system can remain consistent by sacrificing availability when it loses quorum.
This is a major CAP-related behavior.
________________________________________
34. What happens with a 5-node cluster partition?
Suppose:
Partition 1:
A B C

Partition 2:
D E
A/B/C have:
3/5
majority.
They can elect a leader.
D/E have:
2/5
No majority.
They cannot safely elect a new leader.
Therefore:
A/B/C → continue
D/E   → unavailable for committed writes
This is exactly what we want.
We sacrifice one partition's availability to preserve a single authoritative history.
________________________________________
35. Why 2-node clusters are awkward
Suppose:
A
B
Majority:
2
If network partition occurs:
A | B
Neither has majority.
Therefore:
A → cannot safely elect
B → cannot safely elect
Both are unavailable for majority-based committed writes.
This is one reason production consensus systems often use:
3
5
7
nodes rather than 2.
You need an odd number to maximize failure tolerance efficiently.
________________________________________
36. Failure tolerance formula
For a majority-based cluster:
N = 3
can tolerate:
1 failure
before losing quorum.
N = 5
can tolerate:
2 failures
N = 7
can tolerate:
3 failures
In general:
fault tolerance = floor((N - 1) / 2)
for crash failures under a majority model.
But adding nodes isn't free:
•	more network traffic 
•	more storage 
•	more replication overhead 
•	more operational complexity 
________________________________________
37. The subtle difference between election and failover
People often use these terms interchangeably.
But conceptually:
Election
Determines:
Which node has leadership authority?
Failover
The broader operational transition:
Move service responsibility from failed leader to replacement leader.
For example:
A fails
 ↓
B wins election
 ↓
routing metadata updated
 ↓
clients discover B
 ↓
writes resume
The election is one part of failover.
________________________________________
38. Client behavior during election
Suppose:
A = leader
A dies.
During election:
B = candidate
C = follower
There may be a short period where:
no leader
What happens to writes?
Usually:
WRITE
  ↓
cannot commit
  ↓
retry / unavailable
This is intentional.
The system would rather temporarily reject writes than accept conflicting writes from multiple authorities.
This is a fundamental trade-off:
Short unavailability is often preferable to inconsistent committed state.
________________________________________
39. Client retry after election
Suppose client sends:
WRITE X=100
to A.
A dies.
Client gets:
timeout
B becomes leader.
Client retries:
WRITE X=100
Now we're back to the Lesson 2 issue:
Did A process the first request?
The new leader needs a way to determine whether the first operation was committed.
And the application may need idempotency.
So:
Leader failure
       ↓
Election
       ↓
Client retry
       ↓
Duplicate/ambiguous write
These concepts are connected.
________________________________________
40. The hardest scenario: old leader comes back
Suppose:
A = old leader, term 7
B = new leader, term 8
A was partitioned.
Now network heals.
A reconnects.
What should A do?
It receives:
B → A:
term = 8
A sees:
8 > 7
Therefore:
A steps down
A becomes follower
A updates term
A reconciles its log
This is crucial.
The old leader does not automatically reclaim leadership just because it was the original leader.
________________________________________
41. Old leader's stale writes
Suppose A had uncommitted entries:
A:
1 2 3 4 5 6
B's authoritative history:
1 2 3 4 5 7
When A rejoins:
common prefix:
1 2 3 4 5

A:
6

B:
7
A's conflicting entry 6 may be discarded.
The cluster converges toward the new leader's authoritative log.
________________________________________
42. Why this is safe
Because the protocol distinguishes:
committed history
from:
uncommitted proposals
A's entry 6 might never have reached quorum.
Therefore:
entry 6 = not authoritative
B's entry 7, if properly committed, becomes the authoritative history.
This is one of the central ideas behind replicated-log consensus.
________________________________________
43. The complete election state machine
You can mentally model a node as:
                  timeout
                     |
                     v
               +-----------+
               | Candidate |
               +-----+-----+
                     |
              majority votes
                     |
                     v
               +-----------+
               |  Leader   |
               +-----+-----+
                     |
              higher term/
              leader failure
                     |
                     v
               +-----------+
               | Follower  |
               +-----------+
And every node tracks something conceptually like:
currentTerm
votedFor
log
commitIndex
In Raft there are additional state variables, but these are foundational.
________________________________________
44. What currentTerm means
Think of:
currentTerm = 8
as:
"The newest leadership generation this node knows about."
If a message arrives:
term = 7
then:
7 < 8
The message is stale.
If:
term = 9
then the node learns:
"There is a newer leadership generation."
It updates its state accordingly.
This makes term comparison a universal stale-authority detector.
________________________________________
45. Election isn't about detecting the physically dead machine
This is worth repeating because it's a common conceptual mistake.
A consensus algorithm doesn't need to prove:
"A is definitely dead."
It needs to establish:
"A is no longer able to act as the authoritative leader."
Those are different.
If A is alive but isolated:
A:
still running

B,C:
have quorum
B can become leader while A continues running.
The protocol's goal is:
A may be alive
BUT
A cannot safely commit conflicting operations
That is a much more achievable distributed-systems guarantee.
________________________________________
46. Connection to CAP
Now you can see CAP more concretely.
Partition:
A | B C
B/C have quorum.
A does not.
If A continues accepting authoritative writes:
availability ↑
consistency ↓
If A refuses:
availability ↓
consistency ↑
Consensus-based leader systems generally choose:
Do not allow a partition without quorum to make committed progress.
Therefore during certain partitions:
some requests fail
This is deliberate behavior.
________________________________________
47. Election latency
Suppose:
heartbeat interval = 100 ms
election timeout = 300–500 ms
Leader dies at:
t = 0
Follower detects timeout around:
t ≈ 300–500 ms
Election:
~ network round trip(s)
New leader begins heartbeats.
Total failover might be:
hundreds of milliseconds
or seconds depending on system/network/configuration.
During that interval:
writes may fail
reads may behave differently
clients may retry
So leader election has a real application-level availability cost.
________________________________________
48. Why aggressive election timeouts can be dangerous
Suppose normal GC pause:
JVM pauses for 2 seconds
Election timeout:
1 second
The leader is not dead.
But followers stop receiving heartbeats.
They conclude:
leader unavailable
and start elections.
Now your Java application's GC behavior has become a distributed-systems event.
This is why experienced distributed-system engineers care about:
•	GC pauses 
•	CPU starvation 
•	network latency 
•	disk stalls 
•	scheduling 
•	clock behavior 
•	tail latency 
not just average latency.
________________________________________
49. A Java-specific thought experiment
Suppose your leader is a Java process:
Leader JVM
The process is alive.
But:
Stop-the-world GC = 4 seconds
Followers:
heartbeat timeout = 2 seconds
Timeline:
t0  Leader sends heartbeat
t1  JVM enters long GC
t2  heartbeat missed
t3  follower timeout
t4  election starts
t5  new leader elected
t6  old JVM resumes
The JVM thinks:
"I'm alive."
The cluster thinks:
"You are stale."
This is a beautiful example of why process liveness ≠ distributed leadership.
________________________________________
50. The complete leader-failure sequence
Let's put everything together.
                    NORMAL
                       |
                       v
                 Leader A
                 term 7
                  /    \
                 /      \
                v        v
               B          C
A fails or becomes unreachable.
                       A
                       X
                      / \
                     v   v
                    B    C
Follower timeout:
B suspects A
B becomes candidate:
term = 8
B requests votes:
B → C
C grants vote.
B = majority
B becomes leader:
Leader B
term = 8
B sends heartbeats:
B → C
Clients discover B.
Old A eventually reconnects:
A receives term 8
A steps down:
A = follower
term = 8
A synchronizes its log.
That is the basic leader-failure lifecycle.
________________________________________
51. Now the important invariant
After studying all of this, you should have one very strong invariant in your head:
A node must not be able to make conflicting committed decisions merely because it is isolated and still believes it is leader.
That is the fundamental problem election/quorum/term mechanisms solve.
________________________________________
52. The five mechanisms and their jobs
Keep this table.
Mechanism	Problem it solves
Heartbeats	Detect/suspect leader failure
Election timeout	Trigger new election
Terms/epochs	Reject stale leadership
Voting	Choose one candidate
Majority/quorum	Prevent conflicting independent leaders
Log freshness	Prevent stale candidate from becoming leader
Commit index	Distinguish committed history from proposals
Log reconciliation	Repair divergent replicas
These mechanisms work together.
None of them alone is enough.
________________________________________
53. The architecture we've built so far
At this point, our mental model is:
                         CLIENTS
