43. The deeper abstraction
At university/textbook level, you might say:
Leader–follower replication is a topology.
But at distributed-systems engineering level, you should think:
Leader–follower is primarily an authority-management protocol layered over replicated state.
The interesting questions are not:
"Who is the leader?"
but:
Who is allowed to decide?

How is that authority established?

How does authority expire?

How do we reject stale authority?

How do we preserve committed history?

How do clients discover the current authority?

What happens when communication is unreliable?
Those are the real questions.
________________________________________
44. The most important mental model
When designing any leader-based distributed system, mentally separate these four concepts:
             +----------------+
             |   Liveness     |
             | "Who seems up?"|
             +-------+--------+
                     |
                     v
             +----------------+
             |   Election     |
             | "Who leads?"   |
             +-------+--------+
                     |
                     v
             +----------------+
             |    Authority   |
             | "Who may act?" |
             +-------+--------+
                     |
                     v
             +----------------+
             |    Commit      |
             | "What is true?"|
             +----------------+
And then:
Failure detection
       ≠
Election
       ≠
Authority
       ≠
Commitment
A lot of distributed-system bugs happen because engineers accidentally treat these as the same thing.
