Lesson 1 — Why Distributed Locks Exist
1. Start with the real problem
Imagine you have a Java application running on one server.
             ┌──────────────┐
             │ Java Server  │
             │              │
             │ process A    │
             └──────┬───────┘
                    │
                    ▼
              Shared resource
Suppose only one thread should execute:
void processOrder(String orderId) {
    // expensive / non-idempotent operation
}
Two threads could arrive simultaneously:
Thread 1 ────────┐
                 ├──> processOrder("123")
Thread 2 ────────┘
On one JVM, this is straightforward:
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

void processOrder(String orderId) {
    lock.lock();
    try {
        // critical section
    } finally {
        lock.unlock();
    }
}
The lock establishes:
T1: acquire
T1: critical section
T1: release

T2: acquire
T2: critical section
T2: release
So far, nothing interesting.
Now production happens.
________________________________________
2. We add a second server
Your application scales horizontally:
                   Load Balancer
                   /            \
                  /              \
                 ▼                ▼
          ┌────────────┐   ┌────────────┐
          │ Server A   │   │ Server B   │
          │ JVM        │   │ JVM        │
          └────────────┘   └────────────┘
                │                │
                └───────┬────────┘
                        ▼
                  Shared DB
Request 1 reaches A.
Request 2 reaches B.
Both want to process the same logical resource.
Server A                         Server B
   │                                │
   │ process order 123              │ process order 123
   ▼                                ▼
 acquire local lock               acquire local lock
   │                                │
   │                                │
   └──────────────┐    ┌────────────┘
                  ▼    ▼
                DB / shared resource
Here's the important realization:
A JVM lock protects memory inside one JVM.
Server A's ReentrantLock knows nothing about Server B's ReentrantLock.
So this:
// Server A
lock.lock();
doesn't prevent this:
// Server B
lock.lock();
They are two completely independent objects.
________________________________________
3. The fundamental distributed-lock problem
We need a mechanism that both servers can observe.
Conceptually:
              ┌──────────────────────┐
              │  Coordination system │
              │                      │
              │   resource = FREE   │
              └──────────┬───────────┘
                         │
              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
              │                     │
              ▼                     ▼
         Server A               Server B
Now Server A asks:
"Can I acquire lock X?"
Server B asks:
"Can I acquire lock X?"
The coordination system must ensure that they don't both successfully acquire it.
That's the essence.
________________________________________
4. But why do we need a lock at all?
This is where distributed-system thinking starts becoming important.
A lock is generally needed when we have a shared resource plus a non-idempotent critical operation.
For example:
Example 1 — Payment processing
Suppose:
Order #123
Amount = ₹10,000
Two workers accidentally process it:
Worker A ──> charge ₹10,000
Worker B ──> charge ₹10,000
Customer gets charged twice.
________________________________________
Example 2 — Inventory
Stock:
stock = 1
Two requests:
Request A: read stock = 1
Request B: read stock = 1

A: decrement
B: decrement
Depending on implementation, you can end up with incorrect inventory.
________________________________________
Example 3 — Scheduled jobs
You have:
@Scheduled(...)
void generateDailyReport()
But now you have 10 application instances.
Every instance runs the scheduler:
Server A ──> generate report
Server B ──> generate report
Server C ──> generate report
...
Server J ──> generate report
You intended:
exactly one execution
but obtained:
10 executions
A distributed lock is one possible coordination mechanism.
________________________________________
5. The critical distinction: "same code" ≠ "same process"
This is a common source of confusion.
Suppose you have:
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
and deploy this application to:
10 Kubernetes pods
You don't have:
one lock
You have:
Pod 1 → Lock object #1
Pod 2 → Lock object #2
Pod 3 → Lock object #3
...
Pod 10 → Lock object #10
Even though the source code is identical.
Memory isolation gives you:
Pod 1 JVM memory
      ≠
Pod 2 JVM memory
      ≠
Pod 3 JVM memory
This is why horizontal scaling creates the distributed coordination problem.
________________________________________
6. What exactly does a distributed lock promise?
At first glance we say:
"Only one server can hold the lock."
But that's incomplete.
Let's define a lock more formally.
For resource R, suppose processes are:
P1, P2, P3, ... Pn
A basic mutual-exclusion property is:
In plain English:
At any moment, at most one participant should be considered the owner.
That's the first property.
But distributed systems immediately force us to ask:
What does "at any moment" mean when machines have different clocks, messages are delayed, and nodes can crash?
That question will become extremely important in Lessons 4–6.
________________________________________
7. A lock has two fundamentally different responsibilities
This distinction is extremely useful.
Responsibility A — Mutual exclusion
Prevent:
A ──┐
    ├── critical section
B ──┘
from becoming:
A ───── critical section ─────
B ───── critical section ─────
simultaneously.
________________________________________
Responsibility B — Failure handling
What happens if:
A acquires lock
        ↓
A starts work
        ↓
A crashes
Who releases the lock?
If the answer is:
"A calls unlock()"
we have a problem.
A crashed process cannot execute:
unlock();
So the system needs some mechanism to recover from abandoned ownership.
This leads directly to:
Lease + expiration
which we'll study in Lesson 4.
________________________________________
8. Why not just use the database?
Excellent question.
You already have a distributed database.
Why introduce Redis?
For example, we might try:
SELECT *
FROM locks
WHERE resource_id = 'order-123'
FOR UPDATE;
or:
INSERT INTO distributed_lock(resource_id)
VALUES ('order-123');
with a unique constraint:
UNIQUE(resource_id)
This can absolutely be a valid design.
In fact:
A database can be a distributed lock coordinator.
You don't inherently need Redis.
You could build coordination using:
•	PostgreSQL 
•	MySQL 
•	Redis 
•	ZooKeeper 
•	etcd 
•	Consul 
•	a consensus-backed service 
•	specialized coordination systems 
The real question is:
What system provides the atomic state transition and failure semantics required by our lock?
________________________________________
9. The lock is really a coordination problem
This is the conceptual upgrade I want you to make.
Don't think:
"I need a Java lock, but distributed."
Think:
"Multiple independent processes need to agree on ownership of a logical resource."
That's a coordination problem.
For example:
Resource:
    inventory:SKU-123

Participants:
    Server A
    Server B
    Server C

Question:
    Who currently owns the right to modify it?
The coordinator maintains something conceptually like:
inventory:SKU-123 → Server B
Now:
Server A → acquire → DENIED
Server B → acquire → OWNER
Server C → acquire → DENIED
________________________________________
10. Why ordinary networking makes this difficult
Here's where distributed systems become fundamentally different from single-machine concurrency.
On one machine:
Thread A
   │
   ▼
shared memory
   ▲
   │
Thread B
Communication is extremely fast and failure modes are relatively constrained.
Across machines:
Server A
   │
   │ network
   ▼
Server B
Now a request can:
•	arrive late 
•	be duplicated at the application level 
•	be lost 
•	encounter a timeout 
•	arrive after the client has given up 
•	encounter a server crash 
•	encounter a network partition 
And this creates a nasty situation.
________________________________________
11. The "timeout ≠ failure" problem
Consider:
Client A                 Lock Server
   │                         │
   │ ACQUIRE                 │
   │────────────────────────>│
   │                         │
   │                         │ lock granted
   │                         │
   │        network delay    │
   │                         │
   X timeout                 │
Client A says:
"The request timed out, so I didn't get the lock."
But the lock server may say:
"Actually, I granted it."
This distinction is fundamental.
A timeout tells you:
You don't know the result.
It does not necessarily tell you:
The operation failed.
This is one of the most important distributed-systems mental models.
________________________________________
12. A dangerous example
Imagine:
Client A
   │
   │ acquire lock
   ▼
Redis
   │
   │ success
   ▼
Client A
But the success response gets delayed:
Redis ──────X──────> Client A
Client A times out.
It thinks:
"Lock acquisition failed."
Meanwhile Redis says:
"Lock owner = A"
Client B now tries:
B → acquire
Redis says:
DENIED
Everything is actually fine.
But if the system instead assumes the timeout means "definitely didn't acquire", application logic can become incorrect.
________________________________________
13. Distributed lock vs distributed transaction
Another important distinction.
A distributed lock says roughly:
"Only one participant should perform this critical operation at a time."
It does not automatically guarantee:
lock acquisition
+
database update
+
external API call
+
unlock
are one atomic transaction.
For example:
Acquire lock
     ↓
Update PostgreSQL
     ↓
Call payment provider
     ↓
Update another system
     ↓
Release lock
The lock does not magically make all those operations atomic.
You still have:
lock system
      ≠
database
      ≠
external service
This distinction becomes crucial when we discuss fencing tokens.
________________________________________
14. A subtle but very important question
Suppose:
A acquires lock
Then A pauses for 30 seconds because of:
•	GC pause 
•	CPU starvation 
•	network stall 
•	VM suspension 
•	process scheduling 
•	container freeze 
Meanwhile the lock expires.
B acquires the lock.
Now:
A                  B
│                  │
│ owns lock        │
│                  │
│──── pause ──────>│
│                  │
│          lock expires
│                  │
│                  │ acquire
│                  │
│ resumes          │
│                  │
│ "I still own it" │
Now both A and B may execute the critical operation.
This is one of the deepest problems with distributed locking.
Notice what happened:
A did not necessarily crash.
The process simply became slow.
Therefore:
"The lock hasn't expired from my perspective" is not sufficient evidence that I am still authorized to act.
This leads us directly to fencing tokens.
________________________________________
15. Three levels of thinking about distributed locks
I want you to distinguish these levels throughout this course.
Level 1 — API thinking
lock();
unlock();
Question:
How do I call the lock?
Useful, but shallow.
________________________________________
Level 2 — implementation thinking
SET key value NX PX 30000
Question:
How does Redis implement ownership and expiration?
Better.
________________________________________
Level 3 — correctness thinking
Questions become:
What happens if the client pauses?
What happens if the network partitions?
What if the response is delayed?
What if the lock expires while work is still executing?
Can an old owner modify the resource after a new owner acquires the lock?
What guarantees survive crashes?
This is the level I want us to reach.
________________________________________
16. The fundamental distributed-lock model
You can mentally model the system as three components:
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │ Lock Coordinator    │
                 │                     │
                 │ ownership state     │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
               ┌────────────┴────────────┐
               │                         │
               ▼                         ▼
         ┌───────────┐             ┌───────────┐
         │ Client A  │             │ Client B  │
         └─────┬─────┘             └─────┬─────┘
               │                         │
               └──────────┬──────────────┘
                          ▼
                   Shared Resource
There are three distinct questions:
Q1. Who owns the lock?
Coordinator problem.
Q2. Is the ownership still valid?
Lease / expiration problem.
Q3. Can an old owner still affect the resource?
Fencing problem.
These map almost perfectly onto our lessons:
Lesson 3 → ownership
Lesson 4 → validity over time
Lesson 5 → stale owners
Lesson 6 → failure + correctness
________________________________________
17. What a good distributed lock must consider
At minimum:
Mutual exclusion
Can two valid owners exist simultaneously?
Liveness
If the owner dies, can another participant eventually acquire the lock?
Ownership identity
Can we distinguish:
A's lock
from:
B's lock
Expiration
What happens when the owner disappears?
Stale clients
What happens when A believes it still owns the lock after B has acquired it?
Atomicity
Can two clients race during acquisition/release?
Failure model
What happens under:
process crash
network delay
network partition
GC pause
coordinator crash
coordinator restart
clock issues
Resource protection
Even if the lock service says:
B owns lock
can A still perform an operation against the actual resource?
That final question is why fencing tokens are so powerful.
________________________________________
18. A useful real-world analogy
Imagine a physical warehouse.
There is one machine:
EXPENSIVE MACHINE
and multiple workers.
You create one physical key.
Worker A takes the key:
A → key
Worker B cannot use the machine because B doesn't have the key.
That's ordinary mutual exclusion.
But now suppose:
A takes key
A becomes unconscious
manager waits 10 minutes
manager gives new key to B
B starts operating the machine.
Then A wakes up and says:
"I had the key first."
You have a stale owner.
A physical key alone doesn't solve the problem if the machine accepts actions from an old key holder.
A better system gives each authorization a monotonically increasing number:
A → token 41
B → token 42
The machine accepts:
token 42
but rejects:
token 41
That's essentially the intuition behind fencing tokens.
________________________________________
19. Distributed locks are often overused
This is another important architectural lesson.
Sometimes you don't need a distributed lock at all.
For example, instead of:
acquire lock
read row
modify row
release lock
you might use an atomic database operation:
UPDATE inventory
SET stock = stock - 1
WHERE sku = ?
  AND stock > 0;
Then check:
rows affected == 1
Now the database itself provides the concurrency control.
Or use:
SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
or optimistic concurrency:
version = 17
then:
UPDATE ...
WHERE id = ?
AND version = 17;
A good distributed-systems engineer asks:
Can I eliminate the coordination problem rather than build a distributed lock?
That's often the better design.
________________________________________
20. When distributed locks make sense
They become useful when you genuinely need cross-process coordination.
Examples:
Only one worker should run a cluster-wide job
Only one process should perform leader-like work
Only one process should execute a particular expensive operation
Coordinate access to a resource that doesn't itself provide suitable concurrency control
But even then, ask whether the operation can instead be made:
•	idempotent 
•	atomic 
•	transactional 
•	optimistic 
•	queue-based 
•	partitioned by ownership 
Often these approaches are stronger than locking.
________________________________________
21. The mental model you should retain from Lesson 1
Don't remember:
"Distributed locks are Redis locks."
Remember:
                Distributed Lock
                       │
         ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
         ▼             ▼             ▼
    Ownership      Failure        Stale owner
         │          handling          │
         ▼             ▼             ▼
    Who owns it?   Does it expire?   Can old owner
                                    still act?
And underneath everything:
        Multiple independent processes
                     │
                     ▼
              shared resource
                     │
                     ▼
              coordination
________________________________________
22. Interview-level question
Suppose an interviewer says:
"We have 20 Spring Boot instances processing orders. We want only one instance to process a particular order at a time. What would you do?"
A junior-ish answer:
"Use a Redis distributed lock."
A stronger answer:
"First I'd determine whether the database can enforce the invariant atomically, because a distributed lock may be unnecessary."
Even stronger:
"If a lock is actually required, I'd define the failure model and correctness requirement first: mutual exclusion, lock liveness, behavior during client pauses and network partitions, and whether stale owners can still mutate the protected resource. A Redis lock with expiration alone doesn't necessarily prevent stale-owner writes, so I may need fencing or resource-level concurrency control."
That's distributed-systems thinking.

Lesson 2 — Single-Machine vs Distributed Locking
Now we move from “why do we need distributed locks?” to the deeper question:
Why is locking easy inside one machine, but fundamentally harder across machines?
For a 6+ year Java developer, I want you to understand this from memory model → synchronization primitive → process boundary → network → failure model → correctness.
________________________________________
1. First: what does a lock actually do?
At the conceptual level, a lock protects a critical section.
lock.lock();

try {
    // critical section
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
We want:
P1 ──── acquire ──── critical section ──── release
                                             │
                                             ▼
P2 ─────────────────────────────────────── acquire
The key invariant is:
But notice something subtle:
A lock doesn't magically make code safe.
It works because all participants agree to use the same coordination mechanism.
On one JVM, that mechanism can literally be a memory location.
Across machines, memory is no longer shared.
That changes everything.
________________________________________
2. Single-machine locking
Let's start with the simplest case.
             One JVM
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                      │
│  Thread A        Thread B            │
│      │               │               │
│      └───────┬───────┘               │
│              ▼                       │
│        ┌───────────┐                 │
│        │   Lock    │                 │
│        └───────────┘                 │
│              │                       │
│              ▼                       │
│       Shared memory                 │
│                                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
Every thread can access the same JVM memory.
For example:
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

private int counter;
Both threads refer to the same:
lock object
and same:
counter
That's crucial.
________________________________________
3. What does ReentrantLock really buy us?
Consider:
lock.lock();

try {
    counter++;
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
Without synchronization, counter++ is conceptually:
read counter
add 1
write counter
Suppose:
counter = 10
Two threads:
Thread A                Thread B

read 10
                        read 10

add 1
                        add 1

write 11
                        write 11
Final value:
11
instead of:
12
The lock establishes an ordering:
Thread A:
    acquire
    read
    modify
    write
    release

Thread B:
                     acquire
                     read
                     modify
                     write
                     release
Now:
12
________________________________________
4. Why can the JVM do this?
Because threads share an address space.
Conceptually:
CPU Core 1 ──┐
             │
CPU Core 2 ──┼──> shared memory
             │
CPU Core 3 ──┘
Modern CPUs are much more complicated internally because of:
•	CPU caches 
•	cache coherence 
•	memory barriers 
•	store buffers 
•	instruction reordering 
But the JVM and hardware provide synchronization mechanisms that allow Java concurrency primitives to establish the required visibility and ordering guarantees.
This is fundamentally different from two independent servers.
________________________________________
5. Java Memory Model connection
This is an important point for your level.
Consider:
lock.lock();

try {
    sharedValue = 42;
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
Another thread subsequently acquires the same lock:
lock.lock();

try {
    System.out.println(sharedValue);
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
The lock participates in establishing a happens-before relationship.
Conceptually:
Thread A

write sharedValue = 42
        │
        ▼
    unlock()
        │
        │ happens-before
        ▼
    lock()
        │
        ▼
Thread B

read sharedValue
So locking provides more than:
"Only one thread enters."
It also provides memory visibility and ordering guarantees.
________________________________________
6. Now cross the process boundary
Here's the critical transition.
Instead of:
Thread A
Thread B
   │
   ▼
same JVM memory
we have:
Server A                         Server B

JVM A                            JVM B
┌───────────┐                    ┌───────────┐
│ Thread A  │                    │ Thread B  │
│           │                    │           │
│ Lock A    │                    │ Lock B    │
└───────────┘                    └───────────┘
These are different lock objects.
Even if your source code says:
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
on both machines, you have:
Server A → lock object @ memory address X

Server B → lock object @ memory address Y
There is no shared lock state.
________________________________________
7. This is the fundamental difference
Single-machine:
                 Shared memory
                      │
             ┌────────┴────────┐
             ▼                 ▼
         Thread A           Thread B
             │                 │
             └────── Lock ─────┘
Distributed:
       Server A                         Server B
    ┌───────────┐                    ┌───────────┐
    │ JVM       │                    │ JVM       │
    │           │                    │           │
    │ Lock A    │                    │ Lock B    │
    └───────────┘                    └───────────┘
          │                                │
          └────────── Network ─────────────┘
There is no shared memory location that both can atomically manipulate.
Therefore:
A Java ReentrantLock cannot coordinate two JVMs.
Not because Java forgot to provide a distributed version.
Because the problem is fundamentally different.
________________________________________
8. "Why not synchronize on a database object?"
A common thought:
synchronized(databaseConnection) {
    ...
}
That only synchronizes threads that share that same Java object.
Server A:
databaseConnection object A
Server B:
databaseConnection object B
Even if both connections point to:
PostgreSQL
the Java objects themselves aren't shared.
You need synchronization at a shared system.
________________________________________
9. The database becomes a possible coordinator
Now we're getting somewhere.
Suppose both servers talk to PostgreSQL:
Server A ─────┐
              │
              ▼
          PostgreSQL
              ▲
              │
Server B ─────┘
Unlike JVM memory, PostgreSQL has a single distributed-visible state.
So you could potentially use:
SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
or a unique constraint:
INSERT INTO locks(resource_id)
VALUES ('order-123');
If resource_id is unique:
Server A → INSERT order-123 → SUCCESS

Server B → INSERT order-123 → UNIQUE VIOLATION
Now we have a coordination point.
This is the conceptual leap:
Distributed locking requires a shared authority whose state is visible to the participants.
________________________________________
10. Why can't we just use the network directly?
Imagine:
Server A                      Server B

"I have lock!"
        ────────────────────>
Server B could simply reply:
"Okay."
But now ask:
Who decides who gets the lock?
You need some authoritative state.
For example:
resource = order-123
owner = A
That state must live somewhere.
Possible homes:
Redis
Database
ZooKeeper
etcd
Consul
or another coordination service.
________________________________________
11. Single-machine lock has a huge advantage: atomic memory operations
At the CPU/JVM level, we can use operations such as:
CAS
compare-and-swap
Conceptually:
if lock == FREE:
    lock = HELD
performed atomically.
So:
Thread A ── CAS(FREE → A)
Thread B ── CAS(FREE → B)
Only one succeeds.
For example:
Initial:

lock = FREE

A: CAS(FREE, A) → SUCCESS
B: CAS(FREE, B) → FAILURE
That's extremely powerful.
________________________________________
12. Now try CAS over a network
Suppose we do:
Server A                  Server B

read lock = FREE          read lock = FREE
Both see:
FREE
Then:
A → "set owner=A"
B → "set owner=B"
Without an atomic coordinator operation, both can believe they won.
So we need something equivalent to:
CAS(FREE → owner)
but performed by a shared authority.
That's exactly the type of primitive systems like Redis can provide.
________________________________________
13. Why latency matters
A JVM lock might conceptually be:
Thread
   │
   ▼
shared memory
A distributed lock is:
Server A
   │
   │ network
   ▼
Coordinator
   │
   │ response
   ▼
Server A
You have introduced:
•	network latency 
•	serialization 
•	packet loss 
•	connection failure 
•	timeouts 
•	retries 
•	coordinator load 
Therefore a distributed lock is much more expensive than an in-process lock.
This has an architectural consequence:
Don't use distributed locking casually inside extremely hot code paths.
________________________________________
14. The really important difference: failure
On a single machine, imagine:
Thread A
   │
   ▼
holds lock
   │
   ▼
thread crashes
The JVM can clean up certain resources when the process terminates, and the lock exists only inside that process.
But distributed:
Server A
   │
   │ holds lock
   ▼
Server A crashes
What happens to the lock state in:
Redis?
Database?
ZooKeeper?
The lock coordinator is still alive.
It may have:
owner = A
forever unless some recovery mechanism exists.
That's why distributed locks need mechanisms such as:
expiration
leases
session-based ownership
We'll study this deeply in Lesson 4.
________________________________________
15. The network introduces ambiguity
This is where distributed locking becomes genuinely difficult.
Consider:
Server A                Redis

   │                       │
   │ ACQUIRE               │
   │──────────────────────>│
   │                       │
   │                       │ SUCCESS
   │                       │
   │      response delayed │
   │                       │
   │ timeout               │
   X                       │
Server A says:
"I timed out."
But Redis says:
"I successfully granted the lock."
Now A doesn't know the true outcome.
This doesn't normally happen with a local method call in quite the same way.
The network creates uncertainty about whether an operation happened.
________________________________________
16. The "two clocks" problem
Another distributed-system difference.
On one JVM:
System.nanoTime()
and scheduling are local concerns.
Across machines:
Server A clock = 10:00:00
Server B clock = 10:00:02
Even if both use NTP, clocks aren't perfectly synchronized.
So this reasoning is dangerous:
A:
"My lock expires at 10:00:30."

B:
"My clock says it's already 10:00:31."
Distributed lock implementations therefore have to be very careful about what time means and where expiration is evaluated.
________________________________________
17. Local lock vs distributed lock: conceptual comparison
Property	JVM Lock	Distributed Lock
Participants	Threads	Processes/services
State	Shared memory	External coordinator
Communication	Memory	Network
Latency	Very low	Network-dependent
Failure model	Mostly process/thread	Process + network + coordinator
Ownership	Object/state in JVM	External state
Crash recovery	JVM/process semantics	Lease/expiration/session
Memory visibility	Java Memory Model	Protocol-dependent
Atomicity	CPU/JVM primitives	Atomic server-side operations
Stale owner problem	Much smaller	Major concern
Partition handling	Not applicable in same way	Critical
Fencing	Usually unnecessary	Often important
________________________________________
18. But here's a subtle distinction
You might now think:
"Okay, distributed lock = local lock + Redis."
Not quite.
A distributed lock is not simply:
ReentrantLock
        +
Redis
because the semantics are different.
With:
lock.lock();
the JVM can block the thread until the lock is available.
With a distributed lock:
client → network → coordinator
you have to consider:
What if coordinator doesn't respond?
What if response is delayed?
What if client pauses?
What if lease expires?
What if client resumes?
What if coordinator restarts?
What if network partitions?
These aren't edge cases.
They define the correctness of your locking algorithm.
________________________________________
19. Blocking vs leasing
This is another useful distinction.
A Java lock can conceptually say:
"I own this lock until I explicitly unlock it."
A distributed lease often says:
"You may act as owner until time T,
provided the lease remains valid."
That's a fundamentally different ownership model.
Instead of:
ownership = permanent until unlock
we have:
ownership = valid for bounded time
Why?
Because the coordinator needs a way to recover from:
client crash
without waiting forever.
________________________________________
20. The distributed lock state machine
Let's model a basic distributed lock.
                 acquire
                    │
                    ▼
              ┌───────────┐
              │   FREE    │
              └─────┬─────┘
                    │
                    │ A acquires
                    ▼
              ┌───────────┐
              │ OWNED BY A│
              └─────┬─────┘
                    │
          ┌─────────┴──────────┐
          │                    │
       release              expiration
          │                    │
          ▼                    ▼
       FREE                 FREE
Looks simple.
But now add:
A is paused
and:
A's lease expires
Then:
A                Coordinator             B

│ owns lease           │                  │
│                      │                  │
│      PAUSE           │                  │
│                      │                  │
│                      │ lease expires    │
│                      │                  │
│                      │<──── acquire ────│
│                      │                  │
│                      │ B owns           │
│                      │                  │
│ resumes              │                  │
│                      │                  │
│ "I still own it"     │                  │
Now:
A = stale owner
B = current owner
This is the central failure scenario we'll keep coming back to.
________________________________________
21. Why unlock() itself becomes interesting
Consider Redis.
A naive implementation might do:
DEL lock
when unlocking.
But imagine:
A acquires lock
Then:
A's lease expires
B acquires:
B owns lock
Then A finally wakes up and executes:
DEL lock
Oops.
A just deleted B's lock.
Therefore a distributed unlock cannot simply mean:
delete(key)
It must mean something closer to:
delete key ONLY IF owner == me
And that comparison + deletion needs to be atomic.
This is one of the first important Redis-lock details we'll study in Lesson 3.
________________________________________
22. This gives us an important principle
Ownership must be identified, not merely represented by the existence of a lock key.
Bad:
lock = "locked"
Better:
lock = {
    owner: "client-A",
    expiration: ...
}
Then A can say:
release IF owner == client-A
This protects against an old owner accidentally releasing a newer owner's lock.
But even that isn't enough for every correctness problem.
Why?
Because A may still perform operations against the protected resource after B acquires the lock.
That's where fencing tokens enter.
________________________________________
23. Local locking has no equivalent stale-client network problem
Consider:
Thread A holds ReentrantLock
Can another JVM acquire that same object after A's lock expires?
No such concept exists.
The object belongs to one JVM.
The distributed case has:
logical ownership
whose validity can change independently of the client process.
That's why distributed locks require us to reason about time and authority.
________________________________________
24. The deepest conceptual difference
Here is the sentence I want you to remember:
A local lock coordinates threads through shared memory; a distributed lock coordinates processes through messages and an external authority.
That single difference causes almost everything else:
shared memory
     ↓
cheap atomic operations
     ↓
JMM synchronization
     ↓
relatively simple failure model
versus:
network
     ↓
latency + message loss + ambiguity
     ↓
external coordinator
     ↓
leases / expiration
     ↓
partitions + crashes
     ↓
stale clients
     ↓
fencing / resource-level validation
________________________________________
25. Java developer's mental translation
When you see:
synchronized (obj) {
    criticalSection();
}
think:
Who shares obj?
If answer:
Threads in the same JVM.
Fine.
When you see:
distributedLock.acquire();
ask:
Where does ownership state live?
Who arbitrates acquisition?
What happens if the client crashes?
What happens if the network fails?
How long is ownership valid?
Can an old owner still act?
How is release validated?
What happens if the coordinator fails?
Those questions are much more important than the Java API.
________________________________________
26. One more important architecture point
Suppose your requirement is:
"Only one server should update this database row."
Don't automatically design:
Redis lock
   ↓
database update
Ask whether the database itself can enforce the invariant:
optimistic locking
SELECT FOR UPDATE
unique constraint
atomic UPDATE
serializable transaction
For example:
UPDATE account
SET balance = balance - 100,
    version = version + 1
WHERE id = 123
  AND version = 17;
If zero rows are updated:
someone else changed it
You may have eliminated the need for a distributed lock entirely.
This is a very important system-design skill:
Prefer enforcing the invariant at the resource where the invariant actually lives.
________________________________________
27. What Lesson 2 gives us
We can now derive the need for the next lesson.
We need a shared coordinator that supports something like:
ACQUIRE:
    if lock absent
        create ownership
    else
        reject

RELEASE:
    only current owner can release
And we need those operations to be atomic.
Redis is attractive because it provides very fast atomic operations on centralized/shared state.

Lesson 3 — Redis-Based Distributed Locks
Now we build the first practical implementation.
The goal is not to memorize:
SET key value NX PX 30000
Instead, we’ll derive why every part exists, what can go wrong if you remove it, and what guarantees Redis actually gives you.
________________________________________
1. Our problem
We have multiple Java application instances:
                    ┌─────────────┐
                    │    Redis    │
                    └──────┬──────┘
                           │
              ┌────────────┼────────────┐
              │            │            │
              ▼            ▼            ▼
          Server A      Server B     Server C
All servers want to execute:
process(resourceId)
but for a particular resource:
order:123
we want only one owner at a time.
We can represent the lock as:
lock:order:123
________________________________________
2. First naive idea: SETNX
Redis has an atomic operation:
SETNX key value
Meaning:
Set the key only if it doesn't already exist.
So:
SETNX lock:order:123 server-A
If the key doesn't exist:
1 → success
If it already exists:
0 → failure
Conceptually:
Redis:

lock:order:123 = absent

Server A:
SETNX lock:order:123 A
             ↓
          SUCCESS

Server B:
SETNX lock:order:123 B
             ↓
          FAILURE
This is already a useful primitive.
Why?
Because Redis performs the conditional operation atomically.
There is no:
check
then
set
race.
________________________________________
3. Why not just use SETNX?
Because suppose A acquires:
lock:order:123 = A
Then A crashes:
A
│
│ owns lock
│
X CRASH
The Redis key remains:
lock:order:123 = A
forever.
B tries:
SETNX lock:order:123 B
and gets:
0
B can't proceed.
We've created a deadlock-like abandoned lock.
So we need expiration.
________________________________________
4. Add expiration
Conceptually:
lock:order:123 = A
expires in 30 seconds
Now:
A acquires
    │
    │
    ▼
30 seconds
    │
    ▼
lock automatically disappears
If A crashes:
A crashes
   ↓
lease expires
   ↓
B can acquire
This is the first major idea:
A distributed lock commonly behaves as a lease: ownership is valid only for a bounded period.
We'll dedicate Lesson 4 to this.
For now, we need to understand how Redis creates it safely.
________________________________________
5. The dangerous two-command implementation
You might write:
SETNX lock:order:123 A
EXPIRE lock:order:123 30
Looks reasonable.
But there is a race.
Suppose:
SETNX → SUCCESS
and immediately after:
EXPIRE
the application crashes.
Timeline:
Client A                 Redis

SETNX ──────────────────> success

        application crashes

EXPIRE
   ❌ never sent
Now Redis contains:
lock:order:123 = A
with no expiration.
We've recreated the original abandoned-lock problem.
Therefore:
Acquisition and expiration need to be established atomically.
________________________________________
6. Redis gives us the required primitive
Modern Redis supports:
SET key value NX PX milliseconds
For example:
SET lock:order:123 random-owner-token NX PX 30000
Let's break this apart.
________________________________________
7. SET
We're setting:
key → value
Example:
lock:order:123 → 8f3c...
________________________________________
8. NX
NX means:
Only perform the SET if the key does not already exist.
So:
SET lock:order:123 token-A NX
means:
if key doesn't exist:
    create it
else:
    do nothing
Redis performs this atomically.
Therefore two clients racing:
A → SET NX
B → SET NX
cannot both successfully create the same key.
________________________________________
9. PX 30000
This says:
Set the key's expiration to 30,000 milliseconds.
So this one command effectively says:
IF lock doesn't exist:
    create lock
    assign owner token
    attach expiration
    return success

ELSE:
    return failure
That's why this form is so important:
SET key value NX PX 30000
rather than:
SETNX
EXPIRE
________________________________________
10. Why do we need a random value?
This is one of the most important details.
Suppose we stored:
lock:order:123 = "locked"
Who owns it?
We don't know.
Suppose A owns the lock.
Then A's lease expires.
B acquires it:
lock:order:123 = "locked"
Now A wakes up and tries to release:
DEL lock:order:123
A has just deleted B's lock.
That's catastrophic.
So the lock value must identify the owner/lock acquisition.
For example:
lock:order:123 = "uuid-A"
B gets:
lock:order:123 = "uuid-B"
Now ownership is distinguishable.
________________________________________
11. Why should the token be unique per acquisition?
Suppose your Java process is:
Server A
You might be tempted to use:
value = "server-A"
But consider:
A acquires
A's lease expires
B acquires
A tries to release
If A later reacquires, or if multiple execution contexts exist, a process identity alone can be insufficient to distinguish one lock acquisition from another.
The safer model is:
lock acquisition #1
    token = random UUID 1

lock acquisition #2
    token = random UUID 2
So every acquisition gets a unique ownership identifier.
For example:
String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
________________________________________
12. Correct acquisition
Conceptually:
String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

boolean acquired =
    redis.set(
        "lock:order:123",
        token,
        SetArgs.Builder.nx().px(30_000)
    );
If Redis responds positively:
OK
we consider ourselves the current owner.
If:
null
the lock already exists.
So:
A → SET NX PX → SUCCESS
B → SET NX PX → FAILURE
________________________________________
13. But now comes a subtle issue
Suppose:
A owns lock
token = AAA
A's lease expires.
Then:
B owns lock
token = BBB
A eventually calls:
DEL lock:order:123
If we blindly delete:
A → DEL
we destroy B's lock.
Therefore release must mean:
Delete the lock only if the stored token is the token I originally received.
Conceptually:
if redis.get(key) == myToken:
    redis.delete(key)
But there's a trap.
________________________________________
14. The classic GET → DEL race
Suppose A owns:
lock = AAA
A executes:
GET lock
and receives:
AAA
Now before A executes DEL:
A's lease expires
B acquires:
lock = BBB
Then A executes:
DEL lock
Result:
BBB → deleted
Timeline:
A                       Redis                       B

GET lock ───────────────>
        AAA <───────────

                        A expires

                                                  SET lock=BBB
                                                  ────────────>

DEL lock ───────────────>
                        deletes BBB!
This is a classic race.
So:
The comparison and deletion must be atomic.
________________________________________
15. Redis Lua script
A common solution is an atomic server-side script:
if redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
    return redis.call("DEL", KEYS[1])
else
    return 0
end
Conceptually:
             Redis
               │
       ┌───────┴────────┐
       │ GET key        │
       │ compare token  │
       │ DELETE key     │
       └────────────────┘
Because the script executes atomically within Redis's command execution model, another command doesn't interleave between the comparison and deletion.
The operation becomes:
RELEASE(key, token)
meaning:
if currentOwner == token:
    delete
else:
    don't touch it
That's the correct semantic operation.
________________________________________
16. Why the token protects against stale release
Let's replay:
A → token AAA
A's lease expires.
B → token BBB
Now A attempts release:
GET lock → BBB
A has:
AAA
Comparison:
AAA != BBB
Therefore:
don't delete
Perfect.
B's lock survives.
________________________________________
17. Basic Redis lock algorithm
So our conceptual implementation is now:
Acquire
token = random unique value

SET lockKey token NX PX leaseTime
Success:
you own the lease
Failure:
someone else currently owns it
Release
Atomic:
if GET(lockKey) == token:
    DEL(lockKey)
This gives us a reasonable basic Redis lease.
But we're not done.
Not remotely.
________________________________________
18. The biggest conceptual trap
Someone might now say:
"Great. Redis distributed locking is solved."
No.
We've solved only part of the problem.
We have approximately:
                    Redis
                      │
                current owner
                      │
             ┌────────┴────────┐
             ▼                 ▼
             A                 B
But what if A gets the lock and then pauses?
________________________________________
19. The GC pause problem
Imagine:
lease = 10 seconds
A acquires:
T=0     A acquires
Then A enters a long JVM pause:
T=1     A starts work
T=2     A pauses
T=3
T=4
...
T=10    lease expires
Redis removes A's lock.
B acquires:
T=11    B acquires
Then A resumes:
T=12    A resumes
A's Java code might continue executing:
updateDatabase();
But A is no longer the current lock owner.
We now have:
A = stale owner
B = current owner
________________________________________
20. This is the most important diagram in distributed locking
Time ─────────────────────────────────────────────>

A:
     acquire
        │
        │
        ├─────── PAUSE ───────────────┐
        │                              │
        │                              │ resume
        ▼                              ▼
     believes                      continues work
     it owns lock

Redis:
     A owns
        │
        │ lease expires
        ▼
     FREE
        │
        │
        ▼
     B owns

B:
                      acquire
                         │
                         ▼
                     critical work
Therefore:
Lock expiration does not automatically stop the old client from executing code.
This is the bridge from Lesson 3 to Lessons 4 and 5.
________________________________________
21. Why this is fundamentally different from a local lock
With:
lock.lock();
the JVM owns the synchronization state.
A thread can't simply continue after another thread has acquired the same lock unless it has actually reacquired it.
But with a distributed lease:
Redis says:
A no longer owns lock
while:
A's JVM says:
I'm still executing this method
Those two facts can coexist.
This is the heart of distributed locking.
________________________________________
22. Another failure: response delay
Consider:
A                    Redis
│                      │
│ SET NX PX            │
│─────────────────────>│
│                      │
│                 lock granted
│                      │
│<──── response ───────X
│
│ response delayed
A may think:
"I don't know whether I acquired."
But Redis knows:
"A did acquire."
If A retries blindly:
SET NX PX
it gets failure.
That's okay if your application understands that the first attempt may have succeeded.
The broader lesson is:
Timeout means uncertainty, not necessarily failure.
________________________________________
23. Another failure: Redis goes down
Suppose:
A ─────> Redis
         lock acquired
Then Redis crashes.
What happens?
That depends heavily on the Redis architecture and durability/failover configuration.
If the lock state is lost or failover produces a state that does not preserve the required ownership semantics, another client could potentially acquire what appears to be the same lock.
So you must distinguish:
"Redis has a lock key"
from:
"My overall system has a correctness guarantee."
A lock service is part of a larger distributed protocol.
________________________________________
24. Redis lock ≠ transaction
Suppose:
A:
SET lock
   ↓
UPDATE PostgreSQL
   ↓
CALL payment API
   ↓
release lock
Redis doesn't make these operations one atomic unit.
Imagine:
SET lock → success
UPDATE PostgreSQL → success
CALL payment API → success
release → network failure
The business operation may have succeeded even though release failed.
Or:
SET lock → success
UPDATE PostgreSQL → success
JVM crashes
The lease eventually expires.
This is why idempotency, transactions, and resource-level concurrency control remain important.
________________________________________
25. A good Java abstraction
Conceptually, your API might look like:
interface DistributedLock {

    LockHandle tryAcquire(
        String resource,
        Duration lease
    );

    void release(LockHandle handle);
}
The LockHandle should contain:
final class LockHandle {
    String resource;
    String token;
}
Not just:
release("order:123");
Because the release operation needs to know:
Which acquisition am I releasing?
________________________________________
26. Why tryAcquire is often better than pretending it's local
A local:
lock.lock();
can block a thread waiting for ownership.
Distributed systems have more complicated failure semantics.
You often want:
tryAcquire(...)
and explicitly handle:
success
failure
timeout
unknown outcome
rather than hiding network uncertainty behind an innocent-looking method.
________________________________________
27. What exactly have we achieved?
Our Redis design provides a useful basic model:
Atomic acquisition
SET key token NX PX TTL
Only one client can successfully create the lock key while it exists.
Owner identification
token
identifies the specific lock acquisition.
Automatic expiration
PX TTL
prevents abandoned ownership from lasting forever.
Safe release
compare token + delete atomically
prevents one acquisition from deleting another acquisition's lock.
That's already substantially better than:
SETNX + EXPIRE + DEL
________________________________________
28. But what guarantee do we actually have?
This is where I want you to become precise.
Can we say:
"Redis guarantees that only one server will ever execute the critical section"?
No.
Why?
Because:
lock ownership
and:
execution of critical section
are not identical.
A client can:
1.	acquire the lease 
2.	pause 
3.	lease expires 
4.	another client acquires 
5.	old client resumes 
6.	old client continues executing 
Therefore two clients may execute code believing they have authority.
The lock service alone cannot forcibly stop the old JVM.
________________________________________
29. The crucial distinction: authorization vs execution
Think of the Redis lock as an authorization lease:
Redis:
"A is currently authorized."
But Redis cannot physically control:
A's CPU
A's JVM
A's database connection
A's external API calls
Therefore:
authorization
       ≠
physical prevention
This distinction is one of the most important concepts in distributed systems.
________________________________________
30. Where fencing tokens enter
Suppose every successful lock acquisition receives an increasing number:
A acquires → token 41
B acquires → token 42
Now B is newer.
If the protected resource understands fencing:
resource accepts token 42
resource rejects token 41
Then even if A wakes up after its lease expires:
A → token 41 → REJECTED
B:
B → token 42 → ACCEPTED
Now we've protected the actual resource from stale clients.
This is Lesson 5.
________________________________________
31. Redis lock vs fencing token
Don't confuse these.
A normal Redis lock value:
UUID = "550e8400..."
answers:
"Which client/acquisition owns this lock?"
A fencing token:
41
42
43
44
answers:
"Which acquisition is newer?"
The second property is much more powerful for protecting downstream resources.
________________________________________
32. Where Lesson 4 fits
Before fencing, we need to deeply understand the lease itself.
Our Redis command:
SET key token NX PX 30000
implicitly says:
"You are owner for a bounded period."
But now we need to answer difficult questions:
•	What does 30 seconds actually mean? 
•	Whose clock matters? 
•	What if JVM pauses for 31 seconds? 
•	Should the client renew? 
•	What if renewal fails temporarily? 
•	Can renewal make a lease effectively infinite? 
•	What happens if the client is partitioned from Redis? 
•	What happens if the client believes it renewed but Redis didn't? 
•	How do we calculate safe lease duration? 
•	Why does a lock's TTL not automatically bound the duration of the critical section? 
Those are the heart of:
Lesson 4 — Lease + Expiration
________________________________________
33. One important correction to a common interview answer
If someone asks:
"How do you implement a distributed lock with Redis?"
Don't answer only:
SETNX
A stronger answer is:
SET key unique-token NX PX TTL
and release with an atomic:
compare-token-and-delete
Then immediately add:
"However, this is a lease-based lock. Expiration can create stale clients, so if correctness of the protected resource matters under client pauses or delayed execution, I would consider fencing tokens or resource-level optimistic concurrency."
That answer demonstrates that you understand the failure model, not just Redis syntax.
________________________________________
34. Mental model after Lesson 3
You should now have this picture:
                 Redis
                   │
       ┌───────────┴───────────┐
       │                       │
       ▼                       ▼
   Lock state              Expiration
       │                       │
       ▼                       ▼
  owner token                lease
       │                       │
       └──────────┬────────────┘
                  │
                  ▼
              Client A
But:
Redis lease
     │
     │ expires
     ▼
Client A does NOT automatically stop.
That one sentence is the gateway to the next two lessons.
