Lesson 5 — Fencing Tokens
This is the most important lesson in the distributed-lock sequence.
If you truly understand fencing tokens, you understand why:
A distributed lock can be correctly implemented and still fail to protect the resource.
The problem is not necessarily Redis.
The problem is that the lock service cannot stop a stale client from continuing to execute.
Fencing tokens solve this by moving part of the correctness check to the protected resource.
