...
atomically.
Possibilities include:
•	a database sequence 
•	a consensus-backed coordinator 
•	a monotonically increasing counter managed by a suitable coordination service 
•	a transactional database operation 
For example:
CREATE SEQUENCE fencing_token_seq;
Then:
SELECT nextval('fencing_token_seq');
might produce:
41
42
43
Each acquisition receives a new number.
________________________________________
15. Why can't every client generate its own token?
Suppose A says:
token = 100
B says:
token = 100
Collision.
Or:
A → 500
B → 300
but B actually acquired after A.
Now:
300 < 500
even though B is newer.
The resource would incorrectly reject B.
Therefore:
The ordering must come from a shared authority, not from independent client clocks or counters.
________________________________________
16. Why timestamps are not good fencing tokens
You might try:
token = System.currentTimeMillis()
This is tempting.
But distributed clocks are problematic.
Suppose:
A's clock = 12:00:10
B's clock = 11:59:59
B acquires later but generates a smaller timestamp.
Or clocks can move because of synchronization adjustments.
Therefore:
A local wall-clock timestamp is not a reliable distributed ordering mechanism.
A fencing token should be generated by a system that actually establishes the ordering.
________________________________________
17. What if two acquisitions happen concurrently?
Suppose:
A ── acquire ──┐
               ├── coordinator
B ── acquire ──┘
The coordinator must serialize the allocation:
A → 41
B → 42
or:
B → 41
A → 42
Either ordering is fine.
The important thing is:
The system establishes one authoritative order.
This is fundamentally related to serialization/consensus-style coordination.
________________________________________
18. Important: fencing does not replace the lock
This is subtle.
You might think:
"If I have fencing tokens, I don't need a lock."
Sometimes the resource-level fencing mechanism can indeed make the lock unnecessary for a particular invariant.
But conceptually they solve different problems.
Lock
Attempts to ensure:
only one current owner
Fencing
Ensures:
older owners cannot affect the resource
A robust architecture may use both:
             Coordinator
                 │
         acquire lock + token
                 │
                 ▼
              Client
                 │
          request + token
                 │
                 ▼
            Resource
                 │
        validate token
________________________________________
19. Why fencing is stronger than "check if I still own the lock"
Suppose A does:
if (stillOwnsLock()) {
    updateDatabase();
}
Looks reasonable.
But:
T1:
A checks → "yes"

T2:
lease expires

T3:
B acquires

T4:
A executes update
The check became stale.
This is the classic:
check → time passes → action
race.
With fencing:
A → UPDATE ... token=41
the database performs:
validate token
+
perform update
as one atomic operation.
That's much stronger.
________________________________________
20. Fencing at the database boundary
Let's make this more concrete.
Suppose:
orders
has:
order_id
status
fencing_token
Initially:
order_id = 123
status = PROCESSING
fencing_token = 40
A owns:
token = 41
A sends:
UPDATE orders
SET status = 'COMPLETED',
    fencing_token = 41
WHERE order_id = 123
  AND fencing_token < 41;
Success.
Now B:
token = 42
B updates:
UPDATE orders
SET status = 'CANCELLED',
    fencing_token = 42
WHERE order_id = 123
  AND fencing_token < 42;
Success.
A wakes up and sends:
UPDATE orders
SET status = 'COMPLETED',
    fencing_token = 41
WHERE order_id = 123
  AND fencing_token < 41;
Database sees:
current = 42
requested = 41
Rejects.
A is fenced.
________________________________________
21. But there's an important caveat
Suppose A performs an external operation before reaching the fenced database.
For example:
A:
token = 41
   ↓
call payment provider
   ↓
payment succeeds
   ↓
database write rejected
Fencing protects the database write.
It does not magically undo:
payment provider
This is crucial.
Fencing is not a universal distributed transaction mechanism.
It protects operations at resources that enforce the fencing token.
________________________________________
22. Fencing a filesystem
Imagine two workers write to a shared storage system.
A:
token = 41
B:
token = 42
If the storage system understands:
write(file, fencingToken)
it can reject:
token 41
after seeing:
token 42
This is historically one of the classic motivations for fencing.
The protected resource must be capable of enforcing the token.
________________________________________
23. Fencing a message consumer
Suppose a single logical partition should have one active processor.
A:
generation = 41
B later becomes owner:
generation = 42
Messages from A with generation 41 can be rejected.
This resembles patterns used in systems where consumers receive an epoch/generation representing their current authority.
The vocabulary varies:
fencing token
epoch
generation
term
version
but the underlying idea is closely related:
Newer authority supersedes older authority.
________________________________________
24. Fencing tokens and leader election
This connection is extremely important.
Suppose we elect a leader:
Leader A → term 41
Leader B → term 42
A becomes partitioned.
B becomes leader.
A later sends:
request(term=41)
The system says:
currentTerm = 42
41 < 42
REJECT
This is conceptually the same idea as fencing.
You will encounter this pattern repeatedly in distributed systems:
term
epoch
generation
version
fencing token
Different names, similar fundamental purpose.
________________________________________
25. Raft gives you an excellent mental model
In leader-based consensus systems, leaders operate in terms.
Conceptually:
Term 41:
    A is leader

Term 42:
    B is leader
A stale A cannot legitimately act as the leader for term 41 once the system has advanced to term 42.
That is fencing-like thinking.
You don't need to equate:
Raft term == Redis fencing token
—they are not interchangeable implementations—but the conceptual connection is extremely useful.
________________________________________
26. Why Redis's random lock token isn't enough
Let's return to:
SET lock resource UUID NX PX 30000
Suppose:
A → UUID-A
B → UUID-B
You can safely do:
release only if UUID == UUID-A
Excellent.
But if A's UUID is:
550e...
and B's UUID is:
123a...
the resource cannot infer:
B is newer than A
Therefore UUID solves:
"Who owns this particular acquisition?"
but fencing solves:
"Which acquisition is newer?"
Different problems.
________________________________________
27. A complete fenced-lock protocol
Now let's design the whole thing.
Step 1 — Acquire
Client asks coordinator:
Acquire(resource=order-123)
Coordinator atomically determines:
new fencing token = 42
and grants:
lock owner = B
token = 42
________________________________________
Step 2 — Client operates
B sends:
operation(
    resource = order-123,
    fencingToken = 42
)
________________________________________
Step 3 — Resource validates
Resource stores:
lastAcceptedToken = 41
It checks:
42 > 41
Then:
accept
lastAcceptedToken = 42
________________________________________
Step 4 — Stale A appears
A sends:
operation(
    resource = order-123,
    fencingToken = 41
)
Resource checks:
41 > 42
False.
Reject.
That's the entire concept.
________________________________________
28. The resource needs atomicity
This is essential.
You cannot safely implement:
read lastToken
if token > lastToken:
    write
unless the resource guarantees that this check and update are properly synchronized.
Otherwise:
A token 41
B token 42
can race.
You need something equivalent to:
if token > current:
    current = token
    perform operation
as one atomic state transition.
Databases are particularly useful here because transactions and conditional updates provide these primitives.
________________________________________
29. What happens if token 43 arrives before token 42?
Interesting case.
Suppose:
A → 41
B → 42
C → 43
but network delivery happens:
C's request arrives first
A's request arrives later
B's request arrives last
Resource sees:
43
and accepts.
Then:
41 → reject
42 → reject
This is exactly what we want.
We don't care about arrival order.
We care about authority order.
That's an important distinction:
network arrival order
        ≠
logical authority order
________________________________________
30. What if token 43's operation fails?
Suppose C gets:
token = 43
but its operation fails.
The resource has:
lastAcceptedToken = 43
Now B sends:
token = 42
It is rejected.
Is that okay?
Yes, if your protocol defines token 43 as superseding 42.
This illustrates that fencing tokens are about authority, not necessarily successful business completion.
Once a newer authority exists, older authority is stale.
________________________________________
31. Token gaps are fine
You might have:
41
43
47
with missing numbers.
That's usually perfectly fine.
The important property is:
not:
You need monotonic ordering, not necessarily contiguous values.
________________________________________
32. Fencing token vs version column
Let's compare them carefully.
Version column
Usually:
version = 17
Client reads version 17.
Then:
UPDATE ...
WHERE version = 17;
If someone already changed it:
version = 18
the update fails.
________________________________________
Fencing token
Client receives:
token = 42
The resource remembers the newest authority:
lastToken = 42
Older:
41
is rejected.
So both use monotonically changing numbers, but:
Optimistic locking usually protects against concurrent conflicting updates based on resource state; fencing specifically protects against stale distributed authority.
They can also be combined.
________________________________________
33. Fencing + optimistic locking
Imagine:
fencing_token = 42
version = 17
You could require both:
UPDATE resource
SET value = ?,
    version = version + 1,
    fencing_token = 42
WHERE id = ?
  AND fencing_token < 42
  AND version = 17;
Now you're enforcing two different invariants:
fencing token
    ↓
is this actor still authorized?

version
    ↓
has the resource changed since I read it?
This can be extremely powerful.
________________________________________
34. Why fencing changes the correctness story
Without fencing:
A gets lock
A pauses
lease expires
B gets lock
A resumes
A writes
Potential corruption.
With fencing:
A gets 41
A pauses
lease expires
B gets 42
B writes → accepted
A writes with 41 → rejected
The old client can still execute code.
That's unavoidable.
But:
It can no longer perform an accepted operation against a properly fenced resource.
That's the important guarantee.
________________________________________
35. This is a much stronger statement
Notice the wording.
We are not saying:
"Only one process executes."
We are saying:
"Only operations carrying currently valid authority are accepted by the resource."
That's much closer to what a robust distributed system can actually guarantee.
________________________________________
36. What fencing does NOT solve
Fencing is powerful, but not magic.
It doesn't solve:
External side effects
payment API
email
SMS
HTTP service
unless those systems participate in the fencing protocol.
Already-completed operations
If A already sent:
charge ₹100
before being fenced, fencing can't undo it.
Incorrect token generation
If the coordinator generates broken/non-monotonic tokens, fencing fails.
Resource that ignores tokens
If the database/storage/API doesn't enforce the token:
token = 41
has no protective effect.
Business idempotency
You may still need:
idempotency keys
for operations like payments.
________________________________________
37. A very important architecture principle
The component that needs protection must enforce the authority check whenever possible.
Don't rely entirely on:
application:
"I promise I won't continue if my lease expires."
Because application processes can:
•	pause 
•	crash 
•	partition 
•	become overloaded 
•	encounter bugs 
•	receive delayed messages 
Instead:
resource:
"I will reject stale authority."
That's much stronger.
________________________________________
38. Why this is related to epochs
You will encounter the same idea under different names.
For example:
epoch = 17
or:
term = 17
or:
generation = 17
or:
fencingToken = 17
The common conceptual structure is:
old authority = smaller number
new authority = larger number
Then:
request.epoch < currentEpoch
means:
stale
This is one of the most reusable distributed-systems patterns you can learn.
________________________________________
39. Why this is especially important in leader systems
Imagine:
A = leader
A network partition occurs.
B becomes leader.
Now:
A still believes:
"I am leader."

B believes:
"I am leader."
If the resource accepts both:
split brain
Fencing/epochs solve this by making leadership generations explicit:
A → term 41
B → term 42
The resource accepts:
42
and rejects:
41
Now the old leader cannot continue acting as the authoritative leader.
This is a much broader application of fencing than Redis locks.
________________________________________
40. The subtle relationship between locking and fencing
A common architecture is:
             Coordinator
                  │
        ┌─────────┴─────────┐
        │                   │
     ownership          fencing token
        │                   │
        └─────────┬─────────┘
                  ▼
               Client
                  │
          operation + token
                  │
                  ▼
               Resource
                  │
             token check
The coordinator answers:
"Who is currently authorized?"
The token answers:
"Which generation of authority is this?"
The resource answers:
"Will I accept this authority?"
Three different responsibilities.
________________________________________
41. Let's compare the whole evolution
We started with:
Version 1 — Simple lock
SETNX lock
Problem:
crash → permanent lock
________________________________________
Version 2 — Lock + expiration
SET NX PX
Problem:
pause
 ↓
lease expires
 ↓
new owner
 ↓
old owner resumes
________________________________________
Version 3 — Lock + owner token
SET key UUID NX PX
plus:
compare UUID before release
Solves:
old owner accidentally deleting new owner's lock
But doesn't solve stale writes.
________________________________________
Version 4 — Lock + lease + fencing
acquire
   ↓
fencing token = 42
   ↓
operation(token=42)
   ↓
resource validates token
Now stale owners can be rejected.
This is the major conceptual progression.
________________________________________
42. One subtle problem: generating the fencing token and acquiring the lock
Suppose you do:
1. get fencing token
2. acquire Redis lock
A gets:
token = 41
but fails to acquire the lock.
B gets:
token = 42
and acquires.
No correctness problem necessarily, but token 41 was wasted.
That's fine.
But consider:
A gets token 41
B gets token 42
A acquires lock
B fails
Now B has a higher token even though A is the current owner.
If the resource blindly says:
token 42 is newer
you can create a semantic mismatch.
Therefore the allocation of authority and lock acquisition must be designed as one coherent protocol.
This is one reason mature coordination systems often combine:
ownership
+
epoch/generation
inside one authoritative state transition.
________________________________________
43. The important design question
When designing fencing, ask:
Does the token correspond exactly to a successful ownership generation?
You want:
generation 41 → owner A
generation 42 → owner B
not:
token 41 → failed attempt
token 42 → owner A
token 43 → failed attempt
token 44 → owner B
The latter can still work if the semantics are carefully defined, but you must understand what the token means.
The safest mental model is:
Each successful transition to a new owner creates a newer authority generation.
________________________________________
44. Why "Redis counter" isn't automatically a complete solution
You might think:
INCR fencing:counter
Great.
But now ask:
Who owns the lock?
When is the token allocated?
What happens if token allocation succeeds but lock acquisition fails?
What happens if Redis fails between those operations?
What happens during failover?
Can the counter go backward?
Can an old coordinator state reappear?
Fencing is a protocol, not just an integer.
This is a major distributed-systems lesson.
________________________________________
45. The resource-level invariant
A clean fenced-resource invariant is:
Then after acceptance:
This gives us:
token 41 → accept
token 42 → accept
token 41 → reject
token 40 → reject
token 43 → accept
The resource doesn't need to know:
who is A?
who is B?
what happened in Redis?
It only needs:
authority generation
That's elegant.
________________________________________
46. But there's an important nuance
The strict rule:
token > lastAcceptedToken
works particularly naturally when each operation represents a new ownership generation.
But real systems may need slightly different semantics.
For example, a client with token 42 may perform multiple operations:
write #1 token 42
write #2 token 42
write #3 token 42
If the resource literally requires:
token > lastToken
then only the first would pass.
Therefore the real rule is often conceptually:
token >= currentAuthority
with the resource recording the newest authority generation.
For example:
currentToken = 42

request token 42 → valid
request token 41 → stale
request token 43 → advance authority
Exactly how repeated operations are modeled depends on the resource protocol.
The essential property remains:
Older generations must not be accepted once a newer generation has taken authority.
________________________________________
47. This distinction matters in implementation
For example, a database row might store:
current_fencing_token = 42
and operations from the current owner can use:
WHERE current_fencing_token = 42
while the operation that establishes a newer generation transitions:
41 → 42
There are several valid implementation patterns.
Don't blindly memorize:
token > lastToken
as a universal SQL recipe.
Memorize the invariant:
A stale generation must not be able to modify the resource after a newer generation has been established.
________________________________________
48. Fencing and distributed databases
Since you're learning distributed databases, here's the broader connection.
Distributed databases frequently need to prevent:
old replica/leader
from writing after:
new leader
has taken over.
The pattern is:
old leader → epoch 17
new leader → epoch 18
A storage layer checks:
request epoch >= current epoch
If:
17 < 18
reject.
That's fencing.
So fencing isn't merely a Redis-lock trick.
It is a general distributed-systems technique for preventing stale authority.
________________________________________
49. The deeper concept: authority revocation
This is the conceptual upgrade I want you to make.
A lease says:
"A has authority until expiration."
When the lease expires:
A's authority is revoked.
But A may not immediately know.
Therefore:
revocation
    ≠
client awareness
Fencing provides a way for the resource to enforce:
revocation
even when:
client awareness is delayed
That's the deeper reason fencing exists.
________________________________________
50. The complete mental model
At this point, your distributed lock model should be:
                Coordinator
                     │
                     │
             ┌───────┴────────┐
             │                │
         ownership         generation
             │                │
             ▼                ▼
          Client A        token = 42
                              │
                              │
                              ▼
                         Protected Resource
                              │
                              │
                     validate generation
                              │
                 ┌────────────┴────────────┐
                 ▼                         ▼
             token 41                   token 42
               reject                    accept
Now the system has two defenses:
lock/lease
    ↓
attempt to ensure one current owner

fencing
    ↓
prevent stale owners from affecting resource
