________________________________________
27. Linearizability enters the discussion
Now we're approaching real distributed-systems theory.
Suppose the lock service exposes:
acquire()
release()
We may want operations to appear as if they happened atomically at a single point in time.
That's the idea of linearizability.
For example:
A acquire
B acquire
A release
should have a consistent ordering.
But distributed systems have network delays and failures.
So you need to know what consistency model your coordination service actually provides.
A system can provide:
•	linearizable operations 
•	sequential consistency 
•	eventual consistency 
•	weaker semantics 
These aren't interchangeable.
________________________________________
28. Why eventual consistency is dangerous for locks
Imagine two replicas:
R1
R2
A writes:
lock = A
but R2 hasn't seen it yet.
B asks R2:
"Is lock free?"
R2 says:
"Yes."
B acquires.
Now:
A
B
both have authority according to different replicas.
That's why a coordination primitive cannot casually rely on eventual consistency.
For a lock, you generally need a strong enough consistency model for ownership transitions.
________________________________________
29. Lock correctness has two dimensions
A useful mental model:
Ownership correctness
Who is the current owner?
A → owner
B → not owner
Resource correctness
Can a stale owner actually modify the protected state?
A → stale → reject
B → current → accept
A system can have decent ownership tracking but poor resource protection.
Fencing closes that gap.
________________________________________
30. Failure matrix
Let's build one.
Failure	TTL only	Owner token	Fencing
Client crash	Eventually recover	Eventually recover	Eventually recover
Lost release	TTL recovers	TTL recovers	TTL recovers
Stale release deletes new lock	❌	✅	Usually unrelated
Client pauses past TTL	❌ stale writes possible	❌	✅ stale writes rejected
Network partition	❌ potentially split brain	❌	✅ if resource enforces token
Duplicate request	❌	❌	❌
External side effect	❌	❌	❌ unless external resource participates
Coordinator failover	Depends on coordinator	Depends	Depends on token authority
This table is worth remembering.
________________________________________
31. Notice the word "Depends"
This is a senior-level habit.
Avoid statements like:
"Redis locks guarantee X."
Instead say:
"Under these assumptions, this protocol provides X."
For example:
"A lease-based Redis lock can provide temporary ownership, but correctness against stale clients requires fencing at the protected resource."
That's much stronger engineering reasoning.
________________________________________
32. What exactly does a correct distributed lock require?
Let's define requirements.
Requirement 1 — Exclusive current ownership
At a logical instant, there should be one authoritative owner.
Requirement 2 — Safe release
Old owners must not delete new owners' locks.
Requirement 3 — Recovery
Failed owners must eventually stop blocking progress.
Requirement 4 — Stale-owner protection
An expired/old owner must not corrupt the protected resource.
Requirement 5 — Clear authority ordering
The system needs to know which owner generation is newer.
Requirement 6 — Appropriate consistency
The coordination state must have semantics strong enough for the invariant.
________________________________________
33. The "stop-the-world" fallacy
A common misconception:
"When the lock expires, the old process stops."
No.
The coordinator cannot reach into a JVM and freeze its thread.
It simply changes:
currentOwner
from:
A
to:
B
The old JVM may still be doing:
processOrder();
This is why expiration alone is not enough.
________________________________________
34. The fencing solution in one sentence
Don't try to stop the stale process; make the resource reject the stale process.
That's the deepest takeaway from Lessons 4–6.
________________________________________
35. What if the resource itself is distributed?
Excellent question.
Suppose your protected resource is:
distributed database
Now fencing must be understood by the distributed database as a whole.
You can't simply put:
lastToken = 42
on one arbitrary node and assume correctness.
The resource needs a consistent mechanism for enforcing the authority generation.
This brings us back to:
consensus
transactions
quorums
leader epochs
The more distributed the protected resource becomes, the more carefully you must define where authority is enforced.
________________________________________
36. Why database transactions are so useful
Suppose:
fencing_token = 42
and we need to update:
order.status
A transaction can atomically establish:
if token is valid
    update order
    update accepted token
This is much safer than:
check token
↓
network call
↓
update
because the latter introduces a race window.
________________________________________
37. The TOCTOU problem
This is called:
Time-of-check to time-of-use
Example:
if (isLockOwner()) {
    // time passes
    update();
}
Between:
check
and:
use
authority may change.
Formally:
because:
The state may change between the two.
Fencing pushes the authorization check to the resource and makes it part of the resource operation.
________________________________________
38. Java example of the dangerous pattern
Don't think this is safe:
if (redisLock.isOwner(myToken)) {
    database.updateOrder(orderId);
}
Because:
T1:
isOwner() → true

T2:
lease expires

T3:
another client acquires

T4:
database.updateOrder()
The check was true at T1.
It doesn't imply authority at T4.
________________________________________
39. Better model
Instead:
database.updateOrder(
    orderId,
    newStatus,
    fencingToken
);
And the database enforces:
UPDATE orders
SET status = ?,
    fencing_token = ?
WHERE id = ?
  AND fencing_token <= ?;
The authorization is now evaluated where the mutation happens.
That's the important architectural shift.
